/** Generated from the overhaul textbook. Re-run tools/import-overhaul-courses.mjs. Do not edit by hand. */
export default {"id":"note-level-10","number":10,"title":"Link MIDI","skillBand":"Textbook","duration":"From the course textbook","summary":"10.1","modules":[{"id":"note-m-10-1-101","code":"10.1","title":"10.1","blurb":"10.1","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"note-l10-1-1","title":"10.1.1 — What Link is (tempo + optional start/stop)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.1.1 — What Link is (tempo + optional start/stop)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 10 · Module 10.1 — Ableton Link deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can say what Ableton Link always shares and what <strong>Sync Start/Stop</strong> adds on top."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Ableton Link is a technology that keeps devices in time over a wired or wireless network. It is built into all Ableton products and a growing list of third-party apps and hardware. In Note, Link lives in <strong>App Settings</strong> under <strong>Link &amp; MIDI</strong>. From the Set Library, tap the three dots at the bottom left, open <strong>Link &amp; MIDI</strong>, then <strong>Link</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"By default, Link locks tempo. Devices on the same Wi-Fi or LAN join one session and stay in time. <strong>Sync Start/Stop</strong> is optional. When you turn it on, Note also shares playback position: a start or stop on another Link device starts or stops Note at the corresponding point. Leave it off if you only want a shared tempo and you want to launch clips on your own clock."},{"type":"prose","html":"Link is not a file transfer. It does not send a Set, a bounce, or MIDI clips. As of Note 2.0 and Live 12.4, <strong>Link Audio</strong> can stream Note’s audio to Live and Push (send only). That is a later lesson. This lesson is the tempo contract: you and a peer stay in time so a drum loop on Note and a bass line in Live (or another Link app) land on the same downbeat."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open Note to the Set Library and tap the three dots at the bottom left to open <strong>App Settings</strong>.","Open <strong>Link &amp; MIDI</strong>, then <strong>Link</strong>. Read the <strong>Ableton Link</strong> toggle and the two extra options that appear once Link is on: <strong>In-app notifications</strong> and <strong>Sync Start/Stop</strong>.","Read <strong>Connected Peers</strong>. It will say <strong>No peers connected</strong> or <strong>Connected to x peer</strong>.","Write one sentence: Link shares tempo; <strong>Sync Start/Stop</strong> also shares start and stop.","Close Settings without changing anything yet. Repeat the path from memory."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does Link share before you touch any extra toggle? <strong>Answer:</strong> Tempo (devices stay in time on the same network).","What does <strong>Sync Start/Stop</strong> add? <strong>Answer:</strong> Playback position. Start and stop on another Link device start or stop Note at the corresponding time.","Does turning on Link upload your Set to Cloud? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Link is network sync, not transfer."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.2.1 Link","Using Link in Note (Enabling Link; Sync Start/Stop)","Note FAQ p.2 (How do I use Ableton Link)","Note Compatibility (Link and Link Audio)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Open <strong>Link</strong> in Settings. Screenshot <strong>Connected Peers</strong> even if the count is zero. Under the screenshot, write the one-sentence difference between tempo lock and <strong>Sync Start/Stop</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l10-1-1</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l10-1-2","title":"10.1.2 — Enable Ableton Link toggle","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.1.2 — Enable Ableton Link toggle","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 10 · Module 10.1 — Ableton Link deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can turn <strong>Ableton Link</strong> on from <strong>App Settings</strong> and confirm the extra Link controls appear."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Link does nothing until you enable it. From the Set Library, tap the three dots at the bottom left to open <strong>App Settings</strong>, go to <strong>Link &amp; MIDI</strong>, then <strong>Link</strong>, and turn on <strong>Ableton Link</strong>. The Note FAQ names the same control as Settings → <strong>Link</strong>. Both paths land on the same toggle."},{"type":"prose","html":"Once the toggle is on, Link-enabled devices on the same Wi-Fi or LAN connect immediately. The page shows <strong>Connected Peers</strong>: <strong>Connected to x peer</strong> or <strong>No peers connected</strong>. Two further options become available: <strong>In-app notifications</strong> (join/leave) and <strong>Sync Start/Stop</strong>. As of Note 2.0, the same Link menu also holds <strong>Link Audio</strong> settings."},{"type":"prose","html":"Enabling Link may prompt iOS to let Note find devices on local networks. That permission is the next lesson. You do not need Cloud, a Live license, or another paid app to turn the toggle on. A peer count of zero is a valid result: you proved the control works."},{"type":"prose","html":"Musically, this is how you put Note on the same grid as Live, Push 3 (standalone), Move, another iPhone or iPad running Note, or any other Link-enabled app on the network."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Put the iPhone or iPad on Wi-Fi (the same network you will later use with peers).","Open the Set Library. Tap the three dots at the bottom left.","Open <strong>Link &amp; MIDI</strong> → <strong>Link</strong>.","Turn <strong>Ableton Link</strong> on. If iOS asks for local network access, allow it so Link can find peers.","Confirm <strong>Connected Peers</strong> shows a count or <strong>No peers connected</strong>, and that <strong>Sync Start/Stop</strong> and <strong>In-app notifications</strong> are now available.","Leave Link on. Close Settings and reopen the path from memory to prove you can find the toggle again."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Where do you enable Link? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>App Settings</strong> (three dots, Set Library) → <strong>Link &amp; MIDI</strong> → <strong>Link</strong> → <strong>Ableton Link</strong>.","What appears under <strong>Connected Peers</strong> when nothing else is on the network? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>No peers connected</strong>.","Must Cloud be on for Link to work? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Link is local-network sync."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.2 and §2.2.1","Using Link in Note (Enabling Link in Note)","Note FAQ p.2 (How do I use Ableton Link with Note)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Enable <strong>Ableton Link</strong>. Screenshot the Link page with the toggle on and the <strong>Connected Peers</strong> line visible. Keep Link on for the rest of this module."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l10-1-2</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l10-1-3","title":"10.1.3 — Local network permission prompt","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.1.3 — Local network permission prompt","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 10 · Module 10.1 — Ableton Link deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can allow Note to find devices on local networks and name that prompt as the Link permission, not Cloud."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"When you turn on <strong>Ableton Link</strong>, your device may prompt you to allow Note to find devices on local networks. That prompt is iOS local-network access. Link uses the Wi-Fi or LAN so Note can see Live, Push, Move, another Note install, or a third-party Link app on the same network."},{"type":"prose","html":"This is not microphone access (first launch, for sampling) and not the Ableton account login used by Cloud. Refusing local-network access leaves Link on in Settings but unable to discover peers. Using Link in Note says that if a Link-enabled device fails to connect, first confirm every device is on the same Wi-Fi or LAN."},{"type":"prose","html":"If you dismissed the prompt earlier, re-enable it in iOS Settings for the Note app (local network). The extracts do not print the exact iOS label string beyond the in-app prompt. Use the wording on your device. After you allow it, return to <strong>Link &amp; MIDI</strong> → <strong>Link</strong> and read <strong>Connected Peers</strong> again."},{"type":"prose","html":"You care because a jam that “won’t lock tempo” is often a permission or network problem, not a broken Set."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>App Settings</strong> → <strong>Link &amp; MIDI</strong> → <strong>Link</strong>.","If <strong>Ableton Link</strong> is off, turn it on and watch for the local-network prompt. Allow Note to find devices on local networks.","If no prompt appears, assume you already answered it. Open iOS Settings, find Note, and confirm local-network access is allowed.","Return to Note. Confirm <strong>Connected Peers</strong> still updates (zero is fine).","Write one line: local network = Link discovery; Cloud login = Set transfer."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does the Link prompt ask for? <strong>Answer:</strong> Permission for Note to find devices on local networks.","If you deny it, what fails? <strong>Answer:</strong> Peer discovery. Devices on the same Wi-Fi will not appear as connected peers.","Is this the same as signing into Ableton Cloud? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Cloud uses an Ableton account. Link uses the local network."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.2.1 (device may prompt you to allow Note to find devices on local networks)","Using Link in Note (same Wi-Fi or LAN)","Ableton Note.md §1.2 Device Permissions (Local Network Access)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Confirm local-network access is allowed for Note. Screenshot <strong>Connected Peers</strong>. If you had denied the prompt, turn it on and note whether the peer line changes."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l10-1-3</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l10-1-4","title":"10.1.4 — In-app notifications join/leave","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.1.4 — In-app notifications join/leave","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 10 · Module 10.1 — Ableton Link deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can switch <strong>In-app notifications</strong> on so Note tells you when a Link participant joins or leaves."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"After <strong>Ableton Link</strong> is on, Note offers <strong>In-app notifications</strong>. The manual: it sends notifications when other Link-enabled participants join or leave the session. Using Link in Note repeats the same fact. The toggle sits on the Link page next to <strong>Sync Start/Stop</strong>. It does not change tempo and it does not start playback."},{"type":"prose","html":"Turn notifications on when you jam with people who come and go (another phone, a laptop running Live, a Push). You see the room change without opening Settings to stare at <strong>Connected Peers</strong>. Turn them off if you want a quiet session and you will check the peer count yourself."},{"type":"prose","html":"These are in-app notices, not Cloud upload alerts and not “a Set arrived.” Link still requires the same Wi-Fi or LAN. A notification that someone left is not a crash and not a lost file."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>App Settings</strong> → <strong>Link &amp; MIDI</strong> → <strong>Link</strong>. Confirm <strong>Ableton Link</strong> is on.","Turn <strong>In-app notifications</strong> on.","If you have a second Link app or device on the same network, enable Link there, then disable it, and watch Note.","If you have no second device, leave the toggle on and write that you will verify join/leave when a peer is available. Do not invent a notification you did not see.","Close Settings and reopen the Link page from memory. Confirm the toggle stayed where you left it for this session."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What event triggers an in-app Link notification? <strong>Answer:</strong> A Link-enabled participant joins or leaves the session.","Does the toggle sync start/stop? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. That is <strong>Sync Start/Stop</strong>.","Do you need a peer to turn the toggle on? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. You can enable it with <strong>No peers connected</strong>."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.2.1 (In-app notifications)","Using Link in Note (In-app notifications)","Ableton Note.md §1.3 Link &amp; MIDI"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Turn <strong>In-app notifications</strong> on. If a peer is available, have them join and leave once and write what Note showed. If not, screenshot the toggle on and mark the join/leave check as pending."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l10-1-4</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l10-1-5","title":"10.1.5 — Sync Start/Stop","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.1.5 — Sync Start/Stop","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 10 · Module 10.1 — Ableton Link deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can enable <strong>Sync Start/Stop</strong> and state that it shares playback position as well as tempo."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sync Start/Stop</strong> is the second extra option on the Link page. The manual: it synchronizes the playback position as well as the tempo. Start and stop commands from other Link-enabled devices cause playback on Note to start or stop at the corresponding point in time. Using Link in Note shortens this to: send and listen to Start/Stop commands."},{"type":"prose","html":"Leave it off when you only want a shared BPM and you will launch scenes yourself. Turn it on when the room should breathe together: someone hits play in Live, Note starts; someone stops on Push, Note stops. Link without this toggle still keeps time. The toggle is optional on purpose."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sync Start/Stop</strong> is not <strong>Link Audio</strong>. It does not stream sound. It is not Cloud. It does not record MIDI. Map <strong>Start/Stop</strong> to a MIDI button in Module 10.6 if you want a hardware transport; that mapping is local to Note and is a different control from this Link option."},{"type":"prose","html":"Musically, use it for a shared downbeat with a collaborator, and turn it off when you want Note to keep looping while the other app stops."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>Link &amp; MIDI</strong> → <strong>Link</strong>. Confirm <strong>Ableton Link</strong> is on.","Turn <strong>Sync Start/Stop</strong> on.","If a peer is available on the same Wi-Fi, start transport there and confirm Note starts; stop there and confirm Note stops.","If no peer is available, leave the toggle on and write that you verified the control’s location only.","Start a clip in Note, stop from Note, and listen. You still own local play/stop. Repeat the Settings path from memory."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What two things does <strong>Sync Start/Stop</strong> share? <strong>Answer:</strong> Playback position and tempo. Remote start/stop start or stop Note at the corresponding time.","If the toggle is off, does Link still lock tempo? <strong>Answer:</strong> Yes. Tempo lock is the default Link behavior.","Does <strong>Sync Start/Stop</strong> send Note’s audio to Live? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. That is <strong>Link Audio</strong> (Note 2.0 / Live 12.4, send only)."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.2.1 (Sync Start/Stop)","Using Link in Note (Sync Start/Stop)","Ableton Note.md §1.3 (Sync Start/Stop)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Write one sentence you could say to a collaborator: when to turn <strong>Sync Start/Stop</strong> on, and when to leave it off. Then set the toggle for the jam you actually have today."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l10-1-5</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l10-1-6","title":"10.1.6 — Sync Start/Stop persistence across launches","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.1.6 — Sync Start/Stop persistence across launches","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 10 · Module 10.1 — Ableton Link deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can test whether <strong>Sync Start/Stop</strong> stays on after you quit and reopen Note, and you can separate that observation from unsourced claims."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sync Start/Stop</strong> lives on the Link page once <strong>Ableton Link</strong> is enabled. The manual and Using Link in Note describe what the toggle does: it sends and listens to start/stop and syncs playback position as well as tempo. They do not state whether the switch survives force-quit, reboot, or a new launch."},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} The assigned extracts (Manual §2.2.1, Using Link in Note, Note FAQ, Note Release Notes through 2.0.1) do not say that <strong>Sync Start/Stop</strong> persists across launches. Do not treat persistence as a documented product rule. Observe your install."},{"type":"prose","html":"This lesson is a lab on Settings memory. MIDI input sources are documented as remembered (Manual §2.3; RN 1.0.12 last-connected MIDI devices). Last key and tempo are remembered on new Sets as of Note 2.0.1. Those facts are not a license to assume Link toggles behave the same way."},{"type":"prose","html":"You care because a collaborator may expect Note to follow Live’s transport the next day. Check the Link page at the start of every session instead of assuming yesterday’s switch is still on."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>Link &amp; MIDI</strong> → <strong>Link</strong>. Turn <strong>Ableton Link</strong> on. Turn <strong>Sync Start/Stop</strong> on.","Note the state of <strong>In-app notifications</strong> as well, so you have two switches to compare.","Fully close Note (swipe it out of the app switcher) and reopen it.","Walk back to <strong>Link</strong>. Write on/off for <strong>Ableton Link</strong>, <strong>Sync Start/Stop</strong>, and <strong>In-app notifications</strong>.","Repeat once more after a device lock/unlock only (not a full quit) and compare.","Keep your written result. Do not generalize it into a course rule."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What do the extracts actually document about <strong>Sync Start/Stop</strong>? <strong>Answer:</strong> It syncs playback position and tempo; remote start/stop start or stop Note. They do not document launch persistence.","Which related Settings <em>are</em> documented as remembered? <strong>Answer:</strong> Connected MIDI sources (Manual §2.3; RN 1.0.12). Last key and tempo on new Sets (RN 2.0.1).","What do you do at the start of a Link jam? <strong>Answer:</strong> Open the Link page and confirm <strong>Ableton Link</strong> and <strong>Sync Start/Stop</strong> yourself."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.2.1 (Sync Start/Stop behavior only)","Using Link in Note (Sync Start/Stop)","Ableton Note Manual §2.3; Note Release Notes 1.0.12 (MIDI devices remembered — contrast, not proof)","Note Release Notes 2.0.1 (last key/tempo remembered — contrast)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Run the quit-and-reopen test. Write three on/off lines. Label the page “observed on this device / this version,” and record the Note version from <strong>Settings → Help</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l10-1-6</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l10-1-7","title":"10.1.7 — Connected Peers count","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.1.7 — Connected Peers count","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 10 · Module 10.1 — Ableton Link deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can read <strong>Connected Peers</strong> and treat a zero count as a real, useful result."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The <strong>Connected Peers</strong> section on the Link page shows how many Link-enabled participants are currently active in the session. Using Link in Note gives the two messages: <strong>Connected to x peer</strong> when something joined, and <strong>No peers connected</strong> when nothing is found."},{"type":"prose","html":"A zero count is not a failed install. It means Note is listening on the local network and has not found another Link client. Common causes when you expected a peer: the other app’s Link is off, the devices are on different Wi-Fi networks, or local-network permission is denied. Using Link in Note: if a device fails to connect, double-check the same Wi-Fi or LAN."},{"type":"prose","html":"Peers can include Push 3 (standalone), Move, another iPhone or iPad with Note, other iOS Link apps, a computer with Live, and other Link-enabled hardware. The count does not name the peer in the extracts. Do not invent a device list inside the counter."},{"type":"prose","html":"You glance at this number before you trust a shared tempo. If you are alone, screenshot zero and move on."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>Link &amp; MIDI</strong> → <strong>Link</strong>. Confirm <strong>Ableton Link</strong> is on.","Read <strong>Connected Peers</strong>. Write the exact on-screen phrase.","If the count is zero and you have another Link app, put both devices on the same Wi-Fi, enable Link there, and read the count again.","If you cannot add a peer, leave the zero result as your document. Do not fake a number.","Close Settings, reopen <strong>Link</strong>, and confirm the line still matches what you wrote."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What are the two <strong>Connected Peers</strong> messages in Using Link in Note? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Connected to x peer</strong>, or <strong>No peers connected</strong>.","What is the first check when a peer should appear but does not? <strong>Answer:</strong> Same Wi-Fi or LAN, and Link enabled on both sides.","Is a zero count a broken Link install? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. It means no participant is active in the session."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.2.1 (Connected Peers)","Using Link in Note (Connected to x peer / No peers connected; same Wi-Fi or LAN)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Screenshot <strong>Connected Peers</strong>. Caption it with the Wi-Fi network name (or “offline”) and whether a second Link app was running."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l10-1-7</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l10-1-8","title":"10.1.8 — Jam with another Link app on same network","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.1.8 — Jam with another Link app on same network","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 10 · Module 10.1 — Ableton Link deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can put Note and one other Link app on the same network and confirm they share tempo."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Link is built to jam across apps and hardware on a local network. Using Link in Note lists typical peers: Push 3 (standalone), Move, iPhone or iPad with Note, iPhone or iPad with other iOS Link-enabled apps, a computer with Live, and other Link-enabled hardware. The FAQ points at the Link product page for the full app list. This course does not invent that list."},{"type":"prose","html":"Both sides need Link on. Both sides need the same Wi-Fi or LAN. After that, tempo locks. Optionally enable <strong>Sync Start/Stop</strong> if you want shared play/stop. Launch a clip in Note (nearest downbeat) and play something in the other app. You should hear one pulse."},{"type":"prose","html":"Link does not merge Sets. It does not send MIDI notes. It does not replace Export or Cloud. If the other app is Live 12.4+ and you are on Note 2.0+, <strong>Link Audio</strong> can send Note’s audio to Live or Push. That is still not required for a tempo jam."},{"type":"prose","html":"If you do not own a second Link app, document that. You can still enable Link in Note and keep a zero peer count. Do not pretend a lock you did not hear."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Put the iOS device and the other computer or device on the same Wi-Fi.","In Note, enable <strong>Ableton Link</strong>. Confirm <strong>Connected Peers</strong> increments when the other app’s Link is on.","Decide <strong>Sync Start/Stop</strong> on or off for this jam and set it.","Open a Set with at least one clip. Launch the clip. Change tempo in one app and listen for the other to follow.","Stop both. Write success or fail: peer count, tempo lock, start/stop (if you used it).","Repeat the enable path from memory on Note."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What two conditions must be true before a jam can lock? <strong>Answer:</strong> Link enabled on each participant, and the same Wi-Fi or LAN.","What does a successful jam prove that Cloud does not? <strong>Answer:</strong> Shared tempo (and optional start/stop) in real time. Cloud transfers a Set file.","If you have no second Link app, what is an honest result? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>No peers connected</strong>, with that limitation written down."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Using Link in Note (peer types; same Wi-Fi or LAN; Enabling Link)","Ableton Note Manual §2.2.1","Note FAQ p.2 (Link product page pointer)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Run a two-app jam if you can (Note + Live, or Note + another iOS Link app). Write three lines: peer count, whether tempo locked, whether start/stop was on. If you cannot, write why and keep the zero-peer screenshot from 10.1.7."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l10-1-8</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l10-1-9","title":"10.1.9 — Link generally “just works” + KB troubleshooting pointer","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.1.9 — Link generally “just works” + KB troubleshooting pointer","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 10 · Module 10.1 — Ableton Link deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can use the official first checks when Link fails instead of inventing a repair ritual."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The manual’s stance is blunt: Link generally works without issues as soon as it is enabled and provides reliable synchronization in most scenarios. When it does not, the same paragraph points you to <strong>Using Link in Note</strong> and the <strong>Link Troubleshooting</strong> articles in the Knowledge Base. Using Link in Note adds: for Live-and-Link questions, use the Link section of the Live manual plus Ableton’s Link troubleshooting and set-up tips. For apps other than Note, contact that app’s developer."},{"type":"prose","html":"The first Note-side checks in the extracts are small: <strong>Ableton Link</strong> is on; local-network permission was allowed; every device is on the same Wi-Fi or LAN; <strong>Connected Peers</strong> is not stuck at <strong>No peers connected</strong> when a peer should exist. <strong>Sync Start/Stop</strong> is optional and is not the fix for a missing peer."},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not invent firewall ports, Bluetooth pairing steps, or “restart the router three times” as Note doctrine. Those steps are not in the assigned extracts. Send the student (you) to the KB articles the manual names."},{"type":"prose","html":"A working Link session is quiet. You enable it and play. Troubleshooting starts only when the peer count or the tempo lock disagrees with what you can see in the room."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>Link &amp; MIDI</strong> → <strong>Link</strong>. Confirm <strong>Ableton Link</strong> is on.","Confirm local-network permission and same Wi-Fi or LAN for every device you expect.","Read <strong>Connected Peers</strong>. If it is wrong, stop. Do not keep adding toggles.","Open <strong>Using Link in Note</strong> from Help / the Knowledge Base path named in the manual. Follow that article, not a forum guess.","If the other app is not Note, write the developer you would contact. If the other app is Live, write that you would use Live’s Link chapter plus Ableton’s Link troubleshooting guides.","Play one clip. Confirm tempo lock or write the failure in one sentence."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does the manual say Link usually does once enabled? <strong>Answer:</strong> It generally works without issues and provides reliable synchronization in most scenarios.","Where does the manual send you when it does not? <strong>Answer:</strong> Using Link in Note, and the Link Troubleshooting articles in the Knowledge Base.","Who supports a third-party Link app that will not join? <strong>Answer:</strong> That app’s developer, not Note’s MIDI mapping page."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.2.1 (generally works; Using Link in Note; Link Troubleshooting)","Using Link in Note (Live manual Link section; troubleshooting; other-app developers)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Write a four-line first-aid card: Link on, local network allowed, same Wi-Fi/LAN, then open the KB. Keep it next to your peer screenshot."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l10-1-9</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l10-1-10","title":"10.1.10 — Link Audio (2.x): stream Note audio to Live and Push","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.1.10 — Link Audio (2.x): stream Note audio to Live and Push","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 10 · Module 10.1 — Ableton Link deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can state what <strong>Link Audio</strong> sends, which versions it needs, and that Note’s implementation is send-only."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Link Audio</strong> is a Note 2.x / Live 12.4 feature. Release notes for Note 2.0.1: new settings in the Link menu for Link Audio; live-stream Note’s audio to Live and Push. Note Compatibility: as of Live 12.4 and Note 2.0, Link Audio is supported on Note for sending audio only. The FAQ: as of Note 2.0, audio can be sent from Note to linked peers via Link Audio."},{"type":"prose","html":"That last clause matters. Note does not, in these sources, receive a Live or Push mix back over Link Audio. You stream Note’s output out. Using Link in Note also lists <strong>Audio: Share Audio with Live</strong> as a feature you can enable after Link is on, and it dates wireless audio streaming to Live 12.4."},{"type":"prose","html":"Link Audio is not Export Audio (a WAV or M4A file of scenes). It is not Cloud (a Set file in Live’s browser). It is not MIDI. Pre-2.0 Note has no Link Audio settings; do not blend this lesson into a 1.x install."},{"type":"prose","html":"Use it when you want to hear Note inside Live or on Push during a jam without bouncing a file first."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Check <strong>Settings → Help</strong> for the Note version. If it is below 2.0, write that Link Audio is unavailable and stop the audio test.","If you are on 2.0+, open <strong>Link &amp; MIDI</strong> → <strong>Link</strong>. Confirm <strong>Ableton Link</strong> is on.","Find the <strong>Link Audio</strong> / <strong>Audio</strong> settings in that same Link menu (next lesson names the location again).","If Live 12.4+ or Push is on the same network with Link on, enable sharing audio and play a clip in Note. Listen on the receiving side.","Confirm you are sending from Note, not expecting Note to play Live’s output.","Stop the stream. Write success/fail and versions."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Which versions add Link Audio for Note? <strong>Answer:</strong> Note 2.0 (RN 2.0.1 settings; Compatibility: Note 2.0 and Live 12.4).","What direction does Note support? <strong>Answer:</strong> Sending audio only, to linked peers (Live and Push are the named destinations).","Is Link Audio the same as <strong>Export Audio</strong>? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Export writes a file. Link Audio is a live stream."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note Release Notes 2.0.1 (Link Audio)","Note Compatibility (Link Audio send only; Live 12.4 and Note 2.0)","Note FAQ p.3 (Can I send audio from Note to other apps?)","Using Link in Note (as of Live 12.4; Audio: Share Audio with Live)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Write “Note version / Live version / send-only” on a card. If you have 2.0+ and Live 12.4+, stream one clip and note whether you heard it in Live or on Push. If not, document the version gap."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l10-1-10</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l10-1-11","title":"10.1.11 — Link Audio settings location","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.1.11 — Link Audio settings location","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 10 · Module 10.1 — Ableton Link deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can open the Link menu and point to where <strong>Link Audio</strong> settings live on Note 2.x."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On Note 2.0.1, Link Audio is not a separate App Settings chapter. The release notes put “new settings in the Link Menu” for Link Audio. Using Link in Note: after you turn on <strong>Ableton Link</strong> in Settings, additional features include <strong>Audio: Share Audio with Live</strong>. Path from that article: Sets view → <strong>App Settings</strong> → <strong>Link &amp; MIDI</strong> → <strong>Link</strong>, then enable <strong>Ableton Link</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"The pre-2.0 manual (§2.2.1) describes <strong>In-app notifications</strong>, <strong>Sync Start/Stop</strong>, and <strong>Connected Peers</strong> only. Do not look for Link Audio on a 1.x install or in the printed pre-2.0 manual pages. Check <strong>Settings → Help</strong> for the version first."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Link Audio</strong> is still Link: same Wi-Fi or LAN, Link on, then the audio share control. It does not live under <strong>Export Audio</strong> in <strong>Set Settings</strong>, and it does not live under Cloud. Mixing those three locations is the usual miss."},{"type":"prose","html":"You need the location so you can enable a stream without bouncing a file when Live 12.4+ or Push is already in the session."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>Settings → Help</strong> and write the Note version.","If the version is below 2.0, open <strong>Link &amp; MIDI</strong> → <strong>Link</strong> anyway and confirm there is no Link Audio block. Write that as a version gate.","If the version is 2.0+, open <strong>App Settings</strong> → <strong>Link &amp; MIDI</strong> → <strong>Link</strong>.","Turn <strong>Ableton Link</strong> on if it is off. Find the <strong>Audio</strong> / <strong>Link Audio</strong> settings in that menu.","Do not turn on Export. Do not open Cloud. Close Settings and reopen the path from memory."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Which menu holds Link Audio settings as of 2.0.1? <strong>Answer:</strong> The Link menu (<strong>App Settings</strong> → <strong>Link &amp; MIDI</strong> → <strong>Link</strong>).","What must already be on before those extra audio settings apply? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Ableton Link</strong>.","Where will you not find Link Audio? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Set Settings → Export Audio</strong>, and Cloud."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note Release Notes 2.0.1 (new settings in the Link Menu for Link Audio)","Using Link in Note (path; Audio: Share Audio with Live)","Ableton Note Manual §2.2.1 (pre-2.0 Link page contents — contrast)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Draw the path on paper: three dots → <strong>Link &amp; MIDI</strong> → <strong>Link</strong> → audio settings. Open it once without the drawing. Screenshot the Link page on your version."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l10-1-11</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l10-1-12","title":"10.1.12 — When Link Audio beats bouncing a file mid-jam","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.1.12 — When Link Audio beats bouncing a file mid-jam","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 10 · Module 10.1 — Ableton Link deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can choose <strong>Link Audio</strong> for a live send to Live or Push, and <strong>Export Audio</strong> when you need a file."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Two official exits carry sound out of Note. <strong>Export Audio</strong> (Set Settings) renders scenes top to bottom into a WAV (48 kHz, 16-bit) or M4A (48 kHz, 32-bit) file, then you <strong>Share</strong> it. That file is finished media. It does not update when you change a clip. <strong>Link Audio</strong> (Note 2.0 / Live 12.4, Link menu) live-streams Note’s audio to Live and Push. Note’s support is send-only."},{"type":"prose","html":"Mid-jam, a bounce is the wrong tool if the other person is already on Link and you want them to hear the next take now. You would stop, export, wait for the progress bar, preview, Share, and they would import a file that is already old. A stream keeps Note’s output in their Live or Push session while you still play."},{"type":"prose","html":"Use Export when the listener has no Link session: a collaborator without Live, a reference on a phone, a demo you will email. Use Cloud or <strong>Share To…</strong> (<code>.ablbundle</code>) when they need the Set, not the sound. Link Audio does not replace those paths and does not come back into Note."},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} The extracts do not give latency numbers or a “beats bouncing” rule. The choice above is the documented difference between a live send and a file render."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Write two columns: “need a file” vs “need Live/Push to hear Note now.”","Open <strong>Set Settings → Export Audio</strong> on a Set that has clips. Note the format choice and that this will render scenes. Cancel if you do not need the file yet.","Open <strong>Link &amp; MIDI</strong> → <strong>Link</strong>. On 2.0+, find <strong>Link Audio</strong>. Confirm Link is on.","If Live 12.4+ or Push is available, stream one clip instead of exporting.","If not, keep the two-column note. Do not claim a stream you did not run.","Say out loud which path you would pick for tonight’s session."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does Export give you that Link Audio does not? <strong>Answer:</strong> A WAV or M4A file of the scenes, which you can Share to other apps.","What does Link Audio give you that Export does not? <strong>Answer:</strong> A live stream of Note’s audio to Live and Push (send only, 2.0 / Live 12.4).","Does Link Audio send the Set back from Live to Note? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Transfer stays one-way via Cloud or <code>.ablbundle</code>."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note Release Notes 2.0.1 (Link Audio to Live and Push)","Note Compatibility (send only)","Note FAQ p.3 (send audio via Link Audio)","Ableton Note Manual §5.4 and §10.2 (Export Audio)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Pick one real task (collaborator in Live vs listener with no Live). Write which path you will use and why, using only the facts above."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l10-1-12</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l10-1-13","title":"10.1.13 — document peers + tempo lock success/fail","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.1.13 — document peers + tempo lock success/fail","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 10 · Module 10.1 — Ableton Link deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can run a short Link lab and write an honest success/fail record of peers and tempo lock."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"This is the Module 10.1 lab. You already know the controls: <strong>Ableton Link</strong>, local-network permission, <strong>Connected Peers</strong>, optional <strong>Sync Start/Stop</strong>, optional <strong>In-app notifications</strong>, and on 2.0+ the Link Audio block. The syllabus practice is: enable Link and screenshot peers even if the count is zero. The checkpoint is one sentence on <strong>Sync Start/Stop</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"A pass is a written record, not a guaranteed second device. If a peer exists, you also listen for tempo lock. If <strong>Sync Start/Stop</strong> is on, you test remote play/stop. If Link Audio is available and a Live 12.4+ or Push peer exists, you may note a send, but it is not required for this lab."},{"type":"prose","html":"Write what the screen showed. Do not upgrade a zero count into a fictional jam. Link generally works once enabled; if it does not, use the same-network check and the Knowledge Base pointers from 10.1.9."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Record the Note version from <strong>Settings → Help</strong>.","Enable <strong>Ableton Link</strong>. Screenshot <strong>Connected Peers</strong>.","Write yes/no for local-network permission, same Wi-Fi as any peer, <strong>Sync Start/Stop</strong>, <strong>In-app notifications</strong>.","If a peer is connected, launch a clip, change tempo on one side, and mark tempo lock pass/fail.","If <strong>Sync Start/Stop</strong> is on and a peer can start/stop, mark that pass/fail.","Write the checkpoint sentence: <strong>Sync Start/Stop</strong> shares playback position and start/stop as well as tempo.","Close Settings and reopen <strong>Link</strong> from memory to confirm the same states."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What screenshot does this lab require even when you are alone? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Connected Peers</strong>, including <strong>No peers connected</strong>.","What one sentence is the module checkpoint? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Sync Start/Stop</strong> syncs playback position as well as tempo; remote start/stop start or stop Note.","May you skip the peer jam if you have no second Link app? <strong>Answer:</strong> Yes. Document zero peers and still write the checkpoint sentence."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.2.1","Using Link in Note","Note FAQ p.2","Note Release Notes 2.0.1 (Link Audio — optional note only)","Course syllabus Module 10.1 practice and checkpoint"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Save the screenshot and a five-line lab card (version, peers, tempo lock, start/stop, checkpoint sentence). That card is the Module 10.1 deliverable."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l10-1-13</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"note-m-10-2-102","code":"10.2","title":"10.2","blurb":"10.2","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"note-l10-2-1","title":"10.2.1 — Class-compliant requirement","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.2.1 — Class-compliant requirement","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 10 · Module 10.2 — USB MIDI</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can define a class-compliant MIDI controller and know that Note expects that class of device over USB or Bluetooth."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Note works with class-compliant MIDI controllers and keyboards, over USB or Bluetooth. Class-compliant means the device does not need a driver install to work with macOS or iOS. The Knowledge Base: the majority of MIDI keyboards are class compliant on those systems. MIDI input arrived as of Note 1.0.11 (KB); RN 1.0.10 already lists USB and wireless MIDI input."},{"type":"prose","html":"Note does not host AUv3, does not import or export MIDI files, and does not turn a non-class-compliant desktop interface into a supported Note controller via a vendor installer. If a controller requires macOS software to appear as MIDI, it is outside this lesson’s sourced path."},{"type":"prose","html":"Once a class-compliant source is connected and its toggle is on, you play Note’s instruments. Recorded input is pitch, length, velocity, and aftertouch including polyphonic aftertouch. Not recorded: MPE, pitch-bend, sustain."},{"type":"prose","html":"You care because buying or packing a controller for Note is a class-compliance check first, not a brand-name hunt."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>App Settings</strong> → <strong>Link &amp; MIDI</strong> (or <strong>MIDI Settings</strong> on the MIDI Input page) and find the MIDI input list. It may be empty.","Read the name of any controller you own. Check the manufacturer’s iOS/macOS note for “class-compliant” or “no driver.”","If you cannot confirm class-compliance, write that as unknown. Do not assume Note will install a driver — it will not.","Write the recorded MIDI types and the three unsupported types from memory.","Leave the list open. Next lessons add USB, then the per-device toggle."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does class-compliant mean here? <strong>Answer:</strong> No software driver install is required for macOS / iOS.","Which connection types does Note accept for those controllers? <strong>Answer:</strong> USB or Bluetooth (plus network and other apps, later modules).","Which MIDI types will Note not record even from a perfect controller? <strong>Answer:</strong> MPE, pitch-bend, and sustain."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note MIDI Input (Compatible MIDI controllers; Types of MIDI Input)","Ableton Note Manual §2.3","Note FAQ p.3 (class-compliant USB or Bluetooth)","Note Release Notes 1.0.10 (USB and wireless MIDI input)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"List every controller you might plug into this iPhone or iPad. Mark each class-compliant / unknown / needs a driver. Pack only the first group for Module 10.2."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l10-2-1</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l10-2-2","title":"10.2.2 — Connect; device appears in list","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.2.2 — Connect; device appears in list","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 10 · Module 10.2 — USB MIDI</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can plug in a USB MIDI controller and recognize it in Note’s MIDI input list."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"To use a USB MIDI controller, connect it to the iPhone or iPad. Note detects it and adds it to the list of available devices. You then enable the toggle next to the controller’s name. Detection and enable are two steps. A name in the list is not yet input."},{"type":"prose","html":"The list lives with the other MIDI sources under <strong>Link &amp; MIDI</strong> / <strong>MIDI Settings → Input</strong>. Note also lists Bluetooth devices, network sessions, and other apps there. USB devices appear when the cable path is live. Note automatically discovers new devices connected while the app is open (KB, Bluetooth section; USB is the same list)."},{"type":"prose","html":"If the name never appears, the next lessons cover Lightning vs USB-C adapters and powered hubs. Do not skip to mapping. Do not assume the pads on the controller already play Drift."},{"type":"prose","html":"The selected track in a Set arms automatically for MIDI input. That matters after the device is in the list and the toggle is on."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open Note so the app is in the foreground.","Connect the class-compliant USB controller (direct USB-C, or Lightning adapter if required).","Open <strong>MIDI Settings → Input</strong> (or <strong>Link &amp; MIDI</strong> MIDI input). Find the controller name.","If it is missing, reseat the cable, confirm the adapter, and reopen the list. Do not enable a different device by guess.","Leave the toggle off for this lesson. Confirm only that the name appeared.","Unplug and plug in once more. Confirm the name returns. Repeat the Settings path from memory."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What happens when you connect a USB controller? <strong>Answer:</strong> Note detects it and adds it to the available-devices list.","Does appearing in the list start MIDI input? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. You still enable the toggle next to the name.","Which track receives the notes once input is on? <strong>Answer:</strong> The track you have open; it is armed automatically. Open a different track to change the target."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.3.1","Note MIDI Input (Connecting via USB; Recording MIDI input)","Note FAQ p.3"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"If you have USB hardware, screenshot the MIDI input list with the controller named. If you do not, write “no USB controller” and skip to the network or app path later. Do not fake a device name."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l10-2-2</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l10-2-3","title":"10.2.3 — Enable toggle per device","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.2.3 — Enable toggle per device","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 10 · Module 10.2 — USB MIDI</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can turn on the per-device MIDI input toggle and hear that only enabled sources speak to Note."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"After Note lists a USB controller, you still enable the toggle next to that name. The manual’s USB page is two sentences: connect, then enable the toggle to send MIDI to Note. The same pattern applies to Bluetooth, network session, and other apps: each source has its own switch."},{"type":"prose","html":"Multiple names can sit in the list. Only toggles that are on send MIDI. Connected sources are remembered and automatically recognized next time (Manual §2.3; RN 1.0.12). If you do not want a source to stay enabled for future sessions, switch its toggle off before you disconnect or close the app. That is Lesson 10.2.7. Today, practice the on state."},{"type":"prose","html":"Open a track. It arms automatically. Play the hardware. Recording still uses <strong>Capture</strong> (Fixed or Free Length), the same as pads. You are not in a separate “MIDI record mode.”"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Connect the USB controller and confirm it is named in the MIDI input list.","Enable the toggle next to that name only.","Open a Set and open a melodic track (or a Drum Rack). Play keys or pads on the hardware.","If you hear nothing, confirm the toggle, the cable, and that you opened the track you meant to arm.","Disable the toggle, play again, and confirm silence. Re-enable.","Capture one short phrase. Listen back. Close Settings and find the toggle from memory."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does the per-device toggle do? <strong>Answer:</strong> It allows that named source to send MIDI to Note.","How do you record the notes you play? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Capture</strong>, same as on-screen pads. There is no separate MIDI-file record path.","If two controllers are listed and only one toggle is on, which one plays Note? <strong>Answer:</strong> Only the enabled device."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.3 and §2.3.1","Note MIDI Input (USB; Recording MIDI input)","Note FAQ p.3 (MIDI recording via Capture)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Enable one USB source, play a four-note phrase on a Drift or other melodic track, <strong>Capture</strong> it, and leave the toggle on for 10.2.8. If you have no USB device, write that and use Bluetooth, network, or another app in later modules."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l10-2-3</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l10-2-4","title":"10.2.4 — Lightning/USB-C adapters","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.2.4 — Lightning/USB-C adapters","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 10 · Module 10.2 — USB MIDI</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can match your iPhone or iPad port to the adapter the Knowledge Base names."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Wired USB controllers connect through the USB-C port on certain iPads. Other iOS devices need a USB-Lightning adapter. The Knowledge Base says those adapters are available from Apple and from online electronics and audio/video retailers. Adapters with additional power input are also available. The manual’s shorter line: you may need an adapter (for example, Lightning to USB) or a powered USB hub if the controller draws more power than the device can supply."},{"type":"prose","html":"This is a hardware path, not a Note setting. The wrong cable produces an empty MIDI list, not an error dialog the extracts describe. Do not invent a “use Camera Connection Kit” brand requirement beyond what the KB states: USB-Lightning adapter, optionally with extra power."},{"type":"prose","html":"USB-C iPads can take a controller more directly. Lightning iPhones and iPads need the adapter. Power is the next lesson if the device enumerates and then dies, or never appears because bus power is short."},{"type":"prose","html":"You pack the adapter with the controller or you do not have USB MIDI on the road."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Look at the iPhone or iPad port. Write Lightning or USB-C.","If USB-C and the controller is USB-C, connect directly (or a USB-C cable the controller expects).","If Lightning, connect a USB-Lightning adapter, then the controller.","Open Note’s MIDI input list and confirm the name appears.","If it does not, try an adapter that has an extra power input (preview of 10.2.5) or write the miss.","Photograph the port + adapter combination you will travel with."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Which iPads can take a USB controller on USB-C? <strong>Answer:</strong> Certain iPads that have a USB-C port, per the KB.","What do other iOS devices need? <strong>Answer:</strong> A USB-Lightning adapter. Adapters with additional power input exist.","Is the adapter configured inside Note? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Note lists the controller after the physical path works."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note MIDI Input (Connecting via USB; Lightning adapter; extra power)","Ableton Note Manual §2.3.1 (adapter or powered hub)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Write your device port and the exact adapter (or “direct USB-C”) you will use. If you cannot make USB appear, document that and continue with Bluetooth or network MIDI."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l10-2-4</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l10-2-5","title":"10.2.5 — Powered hub when bus power insufficient","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.2.5 — Powered hub when bus power insufficient","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 10 · Module 10.2 — USB MIDI</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can use a powered USB hub when a controller needs more power than the iPhone or iPad can give."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Certain MIDI controllers require more power than an iPhone or iPad can provide. The Knowledge Base: connect those with a powered USB hub. The manual: use an adapter or a powered USB hub if the controller draws more power than the device can supply. Adapters with additional power input are another official option."},{"type":"prose","html":"Bus power is the five volts coming from the phone or tablet port. A large keyboard, a pad controller with many lights, or a class-compliant interface can exceed that. Symptoms the extracts do not catalog in detail; treat “device never stays in the list” or “needs more power” as the hub case, not as a mapping bug."},{"type":"prose","html":"A powered hub has its own wall supply. An unpowered splitter is not the same thing. Note still needs a class-compliant MIDI device at the other end of the hub. The hub does not install a driver and does not add MPE or sustain support."},{"type":"prose","html":"You care so you stop blaming Note when the hardware is starving."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["If the controller already appears and plays, you may not need a hub. Write “bus power enough.”","If it never appears, drops, or the maker says it needs more power, put a powered USB hub between the iOS device (via the correct adapter) and the controller.","Power the hub from the wall. Then connect the controller.","Open Note’s MIDI input list. Confirm the name. Enable the toggle.","Play a note on an armed track.","If you have no powered hub and the controller will not stay up, write that limitation and use Bluetooth, network, or another app."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["When do the sources tell you to use a powered USB hub? <strong>Answer:</strong> When the controller requires more power than iPhone or iPad can provide.","What other power-related accessory does the KB mention? <strong>Answer:</strong> Adapters with additional power input.","Does a hub change which MIDI types Note records? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Still pitch, length, velocity, aftertouch including poly AT. Not MPE, pitch-bend, or sustain."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note MIDI Input (USB Powered Devices; adapters with additional power)","Ableton Note Manual §2.3.1"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Label your controller “bus-powered OK” or “needs powered hub / powered adapter.” Pack the wall wart if it is the second kind."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l10-2-5</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l10-2-6","title":"10.2.6 — Devices remembered next session","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.2.6 — Devices remembered next session","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 10 · Module 10.2 — USB MIDI</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can expect a connected MIDI source to be recognized again next time, and you can check the toggle instead of pairing from scratch."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Connected MIDI sources are remembered and automatically recognized the next time they are used (Manual §2.3). Release notes 1.0.12: last-connected MIDI devices remembered. That covers the USB controller you just enabled, and the same memory applies to other input types in later modules."},{"type":"prose","html":"Recognition is not the same as “always on.” The toggle still decides whether that source sends MIDI. If you left the toggle on, the next session can play immediately after you plug in. If you switched it off before disconnecting (next lesson), the name can return without sending notes until you enable it again."},{"type":"prose","html":"This is not Cloud. It is not a saved mapping bank the extracts describe as a file. Mappings are a separate Settings page (Module 10.6). Device memory means you should not be surprised to see yesterday’s keyboard in the list."},{"type":"prose","html":"Confirm it once so you trust the list after a commute."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Enable the USB controller toggle. Play a note so you know the path works.","Close Note fully. Unplug the controller if you want a cold start.","Reopen Note. Reconnect the controller.","Open the MIDI input list. Confirm the same name is there without a new pairing dance.","Check the toggle state. Play again if it is on.","Write “remembered yes/no” for your version (read <strong>Settings → Help</strong>)."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What do Manual §2.3 and RN 1.0.12 say is remembered? <strong>Answer:</strong> Connected / last-connected MIDI devices, automatically recognized next time.","Does remembering a device force its toggle on forever? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. If you do not want it enabled next time, switch the toggle off before disconnecting.","Is this the same as Cloud remembering eight Sets? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Device memory is local MIDI input. Cloud is Set transfer."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.3","Note Release Notes 1.0.12 (last-connected MIDI devices remembered)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Cold-start the controller once. Screenshot the MIDI list on the second launch. Caption it with the Note version."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l10-2-6</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l10-2-7","title":"10.2.7 — Disable toggle before unplug if you don’t want auto-enable","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.2.7 — Disable toggle before unplug if you don’t want auto-enable","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 10 · Module 10.2 — USB MIDI</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can switch a source off before you unplug or quit so it does not stay enabled for the next session."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Manual §2.3: connected sources are remembered and automatically recognized next time. The next sentence is the control: if you do not want a source to remain enabled for future sessions, switch off its toggle before disconnecting the device or closing the app."},{"type":"prose","html":"That is the whole rule. There is no separate “forget this keyboard” command in the extracts. The toggle is the memory of <em>enabled</em>. Unplugging with the toggle on is how a controller surprises you the next day. Unplugging with the toggle off keeps the name available without sending notes until you opt in again."},{"type":"prose","html":"Use this when you share an iPad, when a second controller should be the only live source, or when you do not want stray notes from a keyboard that powers up in a bag."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Enable the USB controller. Play a note.","Turn that device’s toggle <strong>off</strong> while it is still connected.","Disconnect the controller (or close Note).","Reopen Note and reconnect. Confirm the name is recognized but the toggle is still off and the track is silent.","Enable the toggle only when you want input again.","Repeat the off-before-unplug sequence from memory."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["When must you switch the toggle off to avoid a live source next time? <strong>Answer:</strong> Before disconnecting the device or closing the app.","Does switching the toggle off delete the device from Note forever? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Sources are still recognized; they are just not enabled.","What happens if you unplug with the toggle still on? <strong>Answer:</strong> The source can remain enabled for future sessions."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.3","Note Release Notes 1.0.12 (devices remembered — pair with the toggle rule)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Practice one off-before-unplug cycle. Write the two-step habit: disable toggle, then unplug or quit."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l10-2-7</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l10-2-8","title":"10.2.8 — play melodic track from USB keyboard","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.2.8 — play melodic track from USB keyboard","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 10 · Module 10.2 — USB MIDI</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can play a Note melodic track from a USB keyboard and Capture the phrase."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"This is the USB lab. Chain the last seven facts: class-compliant keyboard, cable or Lightning/USB-C adapter, powered hub if needed, name in the list, toggle on, track armed by opening it, <strong>Capture</strong> to record."},{"type":"prose","html":"Open the desired track. The selected track arms automatically. Play the keyboard. Note records pitch, length, velocity, and aftertouch (including polyphonic aftertouch from supported controllers as of 1.2.1). It does not record MPE, pitch-bend, or sustain. <strong>In Key</strong> still snaps melodic pads; a chromatic keyboard can still play notes outside the scale — {type:'gap'} the extracts do not state that incoming MIDI is forced into Key &amp; Scale. Listen, then edit in the MIDI Editor if a note is wrong."},{"type":"prose","html":"If you have no USB keyboard, do not fake the lab. Write the gap and use Bluetooth (10.3), network MIDI from Live (10.4), another app (10.5), or Magic Keyboard on iPad (10.6.9) as the playable path you actually own."},{"type":"prose","html":"Prefer wired headphones while you play (FAQ: Bluetooth headphone latency). The orange mic icon can still appear; it is expected."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Connect the keyboard. Confirm it in the MIDI list and enable its toggle.","Open a Set. Open a melodic track (Drift, Wavetable, or Melodic Sampler).","Play a short phrase. Listen over wired headphones if you have them.","With transport stopped, play again and tap <strong>Capture</strong> (Free Length), or add a Fixed Length clip first and play into it.","Open the MIDI Editor and confirm notes have pitch, length, and velocity you meant.","Unplug only after you decide the toggle state (10.2.7). Repeat the arm-and-play path from memory."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["How do you arm a track for the keyboard? <strong>Answer:</strong> Open that track. It is armed automatically.","How do you store the performance? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Capture</strong> (Fixed or Free Length), same as pads.","What must you write if you have no USB keyboard? <strong>Answer:</strong> Hardware unavailable, plus which alternate MIDI path you will use instead."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.3, §2.3.1","Note MIDI Input (USB; Recording; Types of MIDI Input)","Note FAQ p.3 (Capture; class-compliant)","Note Release Notes 1.2.1 (polyphonic aftertouch)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Capture one 2- or 4-bar melodic clip from USB, or file the unavailability note. That is the Module 10.2 practice."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l10-2-8</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"note-m-10-3-103","code":"10.3","title":"10.3","blurb":"10.3","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"note-l10-3-1","title":"10.3.1 — Connect to Bluetooth flow","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.3.1 — Connect to Bluetooth flow","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 10 · Module 10.3 — Bluetooth MIDI</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can walk the official Bluetooth MIDI path from <strong>Connect to Bluetooth</strong> to a named device."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Note supports wireless MIDI controllers as well as USB. Manual §2.3.2 and the Knowledge Base share one flow. Open Note. Go to Settings → <strong>MIDI Settings → Input</strong> (KB) or the MIDI input page under <strong>Link &amp; MIDI</strong> (manual). Tap <strong>Connect to Bluetooth</strong>. If Bluetooth is off on the device, you are prompted to allow new connections. Once it is on, a list of available Bluetooth MIDI devices appears."},{"type":"prose","html":"Note automatically discovers new devices connected while the app is open. If your controller is missing, the manual’s first checks are: Bluetooth is on, and the controller is in pairing mode if it needs that. Selecting, pairing, and enabling the toggle are the next three lessons. Do not skip to <strong>Mapping</strong> from an empty list."},{"type":"prose","html":"Bluetooth MIDI is not Bluetooth headphones. Headphone latency (FAQ: prefer wired for playing) is a different radio use. A Bluetooth keyboard or pad still plays Note’s instruments; you still <strong>Capture</strong> to record."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Turn the iPhone or iPad Bluetooth on in iOS Settings if it is off.","Open Note → <strong>MIDI Settings → Input</strong> (or <strong>Link &amp; MIDI</strong> MIDI input).","Tap <strong>Connect to Bluetooth</strong>. Allow the prompt if iOS asks.","Wait for the discovered Bluetooth MIDI list. Write whether your controller is named.","If the list is empty, confirm Bluetooth is on. Do not invent a device. Leave pairing mode for 10.3.2.","Tap <strong>Done</strong> only after you have seen the list. Reopen <strong>Connect to Bluetooth</strong> from memory."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What control opens the Bluetooth MIDI list? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Connect to Bluetooth</strong> on the MIDI input page.","What does Note do with new devices while the app is open? <strong>Answer:</strong> It automatically discovers them.","Is this the same as pairing Bluetooth headphones for monitoring? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. This list is Bluetooth MIDI devices."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.3.2","Note MIDI Input (To connect to a Bluetooth controller)","Note FAQ p.3 (USB or Bluetooth)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Open <strong>Connect to Bluetooth</strong> once. Screenshot the discovered list, even if it only shows nothing yet. Keep that screenshot for 10.3.6."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l10-3-1</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l10-3-2","title":"10.3.2 — Pairing mode on controller","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.3.2 — Pairing mode on controller","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 10 · Module 10.3 — Bluetooth MIDI</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can put a wireless controller into pairing mode when Note does not list it."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"If you do not see the device in Note’s Bluetooth MIDI list, the manual says: make sure Bluetooth is turned on and that the device is in pairing mode, if needed. That “if needed” is the whole sourced rule. Some controllers advertise whenever they are on. Others require a dedicated pair button or a hold on power. The extracts do not publish a per-brand pairing recipe. Use the controller’s own documentation for how to enter pairing mode. Do not invent one here."},{"type":"prose","html":"Pairing mode is on the hardware, not a Note toggle. Note only shows devices it can see. After the controller is discoverable, you select its name (next lesson) and complete the system pair dialog."},{"type":"prose","html":"This is still class-compliant Bluetooth MIDI. A controller that only speaks a vendor wireless dongle on a computer is not this path."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>Connect to Bluetooth</strong> so you can watch the list.","Power the controller on. If it has a pairing control, enter pairing mode now.","Wait for the name to appear. If it does not, power-cycle the controller and confirm iOS Bluetooth is on.","Write the hardware step you used (power on only, or a named pair button). Do not copy a brand recipe from memory unless you actually pressed it.","Leave the controller discoverable for 10.3.3.","If you have no Bluetooth MIDI controller, write “unavailable” and skip the hardware steps."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What two checks does the manual name when the device is missing? <strong>Answer:</strong> Bluetooth is on, and the device is in pairing mode if it needs that.","Does Note have a “pairing mode” switch? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Pairing mode is on the controller.","Where do you get a brand-specific pair gesture? <strong>Answer:</strong> The controller’s own documentation. The Note extracts do not list one."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.3.2","Note MIDI Input (Select the device from the list of discovered Bluetooth MIDI Devices)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Get the controller into a state where its name appears in Note’s Bluetooth MIDI list, or document that you do not own one."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l10-3-2</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l10-3-3","title":"10.3.3 — Select device; pair system dialog","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.3.3 — Select device; pair system dialog","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 10 · Module 10.3 — Bluetooth MIDI</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can tap the controller name in Note and complete the system pairing dialog."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"When the Bluetooth MIDI list shows the controller, tap its name to connect it to Note. Then pair the controller to your device. The manual shows that pair step as a system dialog. After pairing, tap <strong>Done</strong> to return to the main MIDI Input page. The controller now appears as a connected wireless MIDI controller. Input still requires the enable toggle (next lesson)."},{"type":"prose","html":"The pair dialog is iOS, not a Note-designed PIN screen the extracts specify. If iOS asks you to confirm, confirm. If pairing fails, the sourced checks remain Bluetooth on and pairing mode. Do not invent a Note reset code."},{"type":"prose","html":"USB and Bluetooth can both exist in the same list. Pairing Bluetooth does not remove a USB name. Each source still has its own toggle."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>Connect to Bluetooth</strong>. Confirm the controller is listed.","Tap the controller’s name.","Complete the system pair dialog.","Tap <strong>Done</strong> to return to the MIDI Input page. Confirm the controller is shown as connected.","Leave the enable toggle for 10.3.4. Do not map macros yet.","Reopen the page from memory and find the same connected name."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What do you tap to start the connection? <strong>Answer:</strong> The controller’s name in the Bluetooth MIDI list.","What do you tap after pairing to leave the list? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Done</strong>, which returns you to the main MIDI Input page.","Does pairing alone send MIDI into a track? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. You still enable the toggle next to the name."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.3.2 (tap name; pair; <strong>Done</strong>)","Note MIDI Input (Select the device from the list)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Pair once. Screenshot the MIDI Input page with the wireless controller connected (toggle may still be off)."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l10-3-3</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l10-3-4","title":"10.3.4 — Enable toggle in Note","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.3.4 — Enable toggle in Note","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 10 · Module 10.3 — Bluetooth MIDI</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can enable the Bluetooth controller’s toggle so it sends MIDI into the armed track."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The last step on the wireless page is the same as USB: enable the toggle next to the controller’s name to send MIDI to Note. Connected and enabled are two states. The manual’s figure caption is “A Wireless MIDI Controller Enabled for Use With Note.”"},{"type":"prose","html":"Open the track you want. It arms automatically. Play the Bluetooth keyboard or pads. <strong>Capture</strong> stores the notes. Types recorded: pitch, length, velocity, aftertouch including poly AT. Not recorded: MPE, pitch-bend, sustain."},{"type":"prose","html":"The source is remembered next session (Manual §2.3; RN 1.0.12). Switch the toggle off before you close Note if you do not want it enabled next time."},{"type":"prose","html":"Bluetooth MIDI input is independent of <strong>Ableton Link</strong>. Link syncs tempo. This toggle sends notes."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["On the MIDI Input page, find the paired wireless controller.","Enable its toggle.","Open a Drum Rack or a melodic track. Play the hardware.","Disable the toggle and confirm silence. Enable it again.","Capture one short phrase.","Decide whether to leave the toggle on. If not, switch it off before you leave the app."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What is the last step after pairing? <strong>Answer:</strong> Enable the toggle next to the controller’s name.","How do you change which track the Bluetooth notes hit? <strong>Answer:</strong> Return to Session View and open a different track.","Does enabling Bluetooth MIDI turn on Link? <strong>Answer:</strong> No."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.3.2","Note MIDI Input (enable toggle; Recording MIDI input)","Ableton Note Manual §2.3 (remembered sources; disable before close)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Enable the Bluetooth source and play four notes you can hear. If you have no wireless controller, write unavailable and skip to 10.3.6’s alternate path."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l10-3-4</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l10-3-5","title":"10.3.5 — Reliability tips (distance, interference)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.3.5 — Reliability tips (distance, interference)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 10 · Module 10.3 — Bluetooth MIDI</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can use only the sourced Bluetooth MIDI checks, and you can mark distance/interference numbers as unsourced."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The syllabus title asks for reliability tips about distance and interference. {type:'gap'} The assigned extracts do not give Bluetooth MIDI range, wall distance, or 2.4 GHz interference guidance. Do not invent meters or “stay away from the microwave.”"},{"type":"prose","html":"What the sources do say: if the device is not listed, turn Bluetooth on and put the controller in pairing mode if needed. Note discovers new devices while the app is open. Class-compliant Bluetooth MIDI is the supported class. For playing instruments, Ableton recommends wired headphones because Bluetooth headphone latency is noticeable (FAQ; latency article). That headphone warning is not a MIDI-controller reliability spec."},{"type":"prose","html":"If a paired controller drops, return to the official path: <strong>Connect to Bluetooth</strong>, confirm the name, confirm the toggle, confirm the track is open. If Link is also on, remember Link uses Wi-Fi/LAN, not this Bluetooth MIDI radio. Mixing the two troubleshooting pages is a common miss."},{"type":"prose","html":"Honest reliability here is process, not folklore."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Write “no official distance/interference spec” at the top of your notes.","Confirm iOS Bluetooth is on and the controller is powered (pairing mode if required).","Open <strong>Connect to Bluetooth</strong>. Confirm the name. Return and confirm the toggle.","Play while standing near the iPad, then farther away if you want a personal observation. Label any drop as observed, not as an Ableton rule.","If you use Bluetooth headphones at the same time, note the FAQ latency warning for monitoring, separate from MIDI.","Prefer wired headphones for the rest of the module when you are judging timing."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What reliability checks are actually sourced? <strong>Answer:</strong> Bluetooth on; pairing mode if needed; device listed; toggle on; track open.","What must you not teach as Note doctrine? <strong>Answer:</strong> Specific distance or interference numbers the extracts do not state.","Why prefer wired headphones while testing MIDI timing? <strong>Answer:</strong> FAQ: Bluetooth headphone latency can delay what you hear. That is monitoring, not MIDI range."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.3.2","Note MIDI Input (Bluetooth flow)","Note FAQ p.2 (Bluetooth headphones / latency)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Write a four-line card that contains only sourced checks. Add one optional “observed on my hardware” line if a drop happens, clearly labeled as observation."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l10-3-5</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l10-3-6","title":"10.3.6 — Bluetooth pad or keyboard into Drum Rack","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.3.6 — Bluetooth pad or keyboard into Drum Rack","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 10 · Module 10.3 — Bluetooth MIDI</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can play a Drum Rack from a paired Bluetooth pad or keyboard and Capture the hits."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"This is the Bluetooth lab. Pair the controller, enable its toggle, open a Drum Rack track (it arms automatically), and play. Drum Rack is 16 pads, each a Drum Sampler. Incoming MIDI hits the kit the same way on-screen pads do. <strong>Capture</strong> stores the clip. Fixed Length (add a clip, then play) or Free Length (play, then <strong>Capture</strong>) both work. Note Repeat still works in both modes if you want it."},{"type":"prose","html":"Recorded types remain pitch, length, velocity, aftertouch including poly AT. Sustain and pitch-bend are not supported, which matters less on a kit than on a lead but is still true."},{"type":"prose","html":"If you have no Bluetooth MIDI controller, document unavailability. Use USB (10.2), network from Live (10.4), another app (10.5), or on-screen pads. Do not fabricate a wireless session."},{"type":"prose","html":"Syllabus practice: connect once or write that you cannot."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Complete <strong>Connect to Bluetooth</strong> → select → pair → <strong>Done</strong> → enable toggle.","Open a Set and open a Drum Rack track.","Play kick, snare, and hat from the hardware (pads or keyboard notes that hit those cells).","Capture a 1- or 2-bar loop (Fixed or Free).","Open the MIDI Editor and confirm the hits. Adjust velocity if needed.","Decide the toggle state before you quit. Repeat open-track-and-play from memory."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["How do you arm the Drum Rack for Bluetooth MIDI? <strong>Answer:</strong> Open that track. It is armed automatically.","What if you own no Bluetooth MIDI hardware? <strong>Answer:</strong> Write unavailable and use another sourced input path. Do not fake the lab.","Does Bluetooth MIDI bypass <strong>Capture</strong>? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Recording is still <strong>Capture</strong>."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.3.2, §2.3","Note MIDI Input (Bluetooth flow; Recording; Types of MIDI Input)","Capture in Note (Fixed / Free; Note Repeat)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Capture one Drum Rack clip from Bluetooth, or file the unavailability note. That is the Module 10.3 practice."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l10-3-6</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"note-m-10-4-104","code":"10.4","title":"10.4","blurb":"10.4","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"note-l10-4-1","title":"10.4.1 — Same Wi-Fi network","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.4.1 — Same Wi-Fi network","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 10 · Module 10.4 — Network MIDI from Mac / Live</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can put the Mac and the iOS device on the same Wi-Fi before you create a virtual MIDI network."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Note can receive MIDI from Mac computers on the same Wi-Fi network via a virtual MIDI network or Bluetooth. The same-network requirement is the first constraint. This is the same idea as Link’s “same Wi-Fi or LAN,” but the feature is different: here the Mac sends MIDI notes into Note’s instruments. Link would only share tempo (and optional start/stop)."},{"type":"prose","html":"If the phone is on cellular and the Mac is on studio Wi-Fi, the network session will not see the device. If one side is on a guest network that isolates clients, the extracts do not diagnose that case — stay on one LAN you control."},{"type":"prose","html":"Cloud also wants a network, but Cloud is an Ableton account transfer of Sets. Network MIDI is a live note cable over the LAN. Do not sign into Cloud to “fix” a missing <strong>Network Session</strong> toggle."},{"type":"prose","html":"You care because every later step in 10.4 fails quietly if the Wi-Fi is split."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["On the iPhone or iPad, open iOS <strong>Settings → Wi-Fi</strong> and write the network name.","On the Mac, open Wi-Fi and write the network name. They must match.","Open Note. You do not need Link on for this module unless you also want tempo lock.","Open <strong>MIDI Settings</strong> / <strong>Link &amp; MIDI</strong> and look ahead for <strong>Network Session 1</strong>. Leave it for 10.4.3.","If the names differ, join the same network now. Do not continue on two SSIDs."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What network condition does Manual §2.3.3 require? <strong>Answer:</strong> The Mac and Note are on the same Wi-Fi network.","Is this the same feature as Ableton Link? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Link syncs tempo. Network MIDI sends notes from the Mac into Note.","Does Cloud login replace same-Wi-Fi for this path? <strong>Answer:</strong> No."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.3.3","Note MIDI Input (To allow input via Network)","Using Link in Note (same Wi-Fi or LAN — related network check, different feature)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Write the SSID used by both devices. If you have no Mac on that network, mark Module 10.4 as hardware-blocked and still read the remaining lessons."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l10-4-1</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l10-4-2","title":"10.4.2 — Create network session on Mac","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.4.2 — Create network session on Mac","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 10 · Module 10.4 — Network MIDI from Mac / Live</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can create a virtual MIDI network session on the Mac so Note has something to join."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"To send MIDI from Live to Note, create a network session on the Mac and connect to your device. The Knowledge Base points at Apple’s “Setting up a virtual MIDI network” for the Mac-side clicks. The Note manual assumes that session exists, then tells you to enable <strong>Network Session 1</strong> in Note."},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} The assigned extracts do not reprint Apple’s Audio MIDI Setup menu path. Do not invent button names. Use Apple’s virtual MIDI network instructions. Typical tools live in Audio MIDI Setup on macOS; confirm on your system."},{"type":"prose","html":"This session is macOS infrastructure. It is not an Ableton Cloud session and not Link. Live will later use it as a MIDI output port (10.4.4–10.4.5)."},{"type":"prose","html":"Without this session, Note’s <strong>Network Session 1</strong> toggle has nothing to receive."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Keep the Mac and iOS device on the same Wi-Fi (10.4.1).","On the Mac, follow Apple’s virtual MIDI network setup. Create a session and connect to the iOS device.","Write the session name you see on the Mac.","Do not open Live’s routing yet. Confirm only that the session exists and the device is invited/connected on the Mac side.","Leave the session running for 10.4.3.","If you have no Mac, write blocked and skip the live test."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What must exist on the Mac before Note can receive network MIDI? <strong>Answer:</strong> A virtual MIDI network session, with the iOS device connected.","Where do the extracts send you for Mac setup detail? <strong>Answer:</strong> Setting up a virtual MIDI network (Apple / KB pointer in Note MIDI Input).","Is the network session a Cloud upload? <strong>Answer:</strong> No."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.3.3","Note MIDI Input (To allow input via Network; Setting up a virtual MIDI network)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Create or confirm one network session on the Mac. Write its name. Keep it alive through 10.4.7."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l10-4-2</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l10-4-3","title":"10.4.3 — Enable Network Session in Note","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.4.3 — Enable Network Session in Note","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 10 · Module 10.4 — Network MIDI from Mac / Live</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can enable <strong>Network Session 1</strong> in Note so the Mac session can send MIDI in."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"In Note, enable the <strong>Network Session 1</strong> toggle to receive MIDI. The manual’s caption: “Enable Network Session 1 to Receive MIDI from a Mac.” The toggle sits with the other MIDI input sources. Same pattern as USB and Bluetooth: the source must be present, then you opt in."},{"type":"prose","html":"If the toggle is missing or inert, the usual sourced checks are: same Wi-Fi, Mac session created, device connected on the Mac. The extracts do not describe extra Note preferences for renaming the session. Use <strong>Network Session 1</strong> as labeled."},{"type":"prose","html":"Enabling the toggle does not route Live yet. Live still needs <strong>Track</strong> enabled on that output port and a MIDI track whose output is the network session. Those are the next two lessons."},{"type":"prose","html":"MIDI types and <strong>Capture</strong> rules do not change because the notes came over Wi-Fi."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Confirm the Mac network session is running and the iOS device is on that Wi-Fi.","Open Note → MIDI input list.","Enable <strong>Network Session 1</strong>.","Leave USB/Bluetooth toggles off if you want a clean test, or leave them as they are and remember which source you trust.","Open a track so it is armed. You will not hear Live until 10.4.4–10.4.6.","Close Settings and find <strong>Network Session 1</strong> from memory."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What toggle receives Mac network MIDI? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Network Session 1</strong>.","Does enabling it play Live clips by itself? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Live must send to that port (next lessons).","What if <strong>Network Session 1</strong> never appears? <strong>Answer:</strong> Recheck same Wi-Fi and the Mac session. The extracts do not add another Note switch."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.3.3","Note MIDI Input (enable via Network)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Screenshot the MIDI input page with <strong>Network Session 1</strong> on. Keep the Mac session running."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l10-4-3</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l10-4-4","title":"10.4.4 — Live Settings: Track enable on network output port","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.4.4 — Live Settings: Track enable on network output port","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 10 · Module 10.4 — Network MIDI from Mac / Live</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can enable <strong>Track</strong> on the network session’s output port in Live’s Settings."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"After the Mac session exists and Note’s <strong>Network Session 1</strong> is on, open Live’s Settings and enable <strong>Track</strong> for the network session’s output port. The manual’s caption: “Enable Track for the Output Port to Send MIDI Out of Live.”"},{"type":"prose","html":"This is a Live MIDI port permission, not a Note Cloud setting and not Link. If <strong>Track</strong> is off, Live will not send notes to that port even if a clip is playing. You still have to route a specific MIDI track’s output to the session in the next lesson. Think of this step as unlocking the door; the next step aims the track through it."},{"type":"prose","html":"Live must be 11.2.5+ to <em>open Note Sets</em>. That version floor is about file compatibility, not a documented requirement for this MIDI port. Use whatever Live you have for the port test, but Gate J still needs 11.2.5+ to open the Set."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open Live on the same Mac that hosts the network session.","Open Live’s Settings / Preferences and find the MIDI port list.","Find the network session output port. Enable <strong>Track</strong>.","Leave <strong>Sync</strong> and other columns alone unless you already know you need them. The extract names <strong>Track</strong>.","Do not play a clip yet. Confirm the checkbox is on.","Write which port name you enabled."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Which Live Settings switch does the manual name? <strong>Answer:</strong> Enable <strong>Track</strong> for the network session’s output port.","What does that allow? <strong>Answer:</strong> Live to send MIDI out that port.","Is this the same as <strong>Show Cloud</strong> in Live’s Library settings? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Cloud is Set transfer. This is a MIDI output port."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.3.3 (Enable Track for the Output Port)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Enable <strong>Track</strong> on the network output. Screenshot or write the port name. Keep Live open for 10.4.5."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l10-4-4</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l10-4-5","title":"10.4.5 — Route Live MIDI track output to network session","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.4.5 — Route Live MIDI track output to network session","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 10 · Module 10.4 — Network MIDI from Mac / Live</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can point a Live MIDI track’s output at the network session so its notes leave the Mac."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"After <strong>Track</strong> is enabled on the port, route a MIDI track’s output to the network session. The manual’s caption: “Route the MIDI Output of a Track to the Network Session.” In Live that is the track’s MIDI To / output chooser aimed at the session you created in Audio MIDI Setup."},{"type":"prose","html":"The notes that leave that track — from the clip or from Live’s MIDI input — are what Note will receive. A Live audio track will not feed this path. A MIDI track whose output is still “To All” or a plugin instrument on the same track may not leave the box; send the MIDI to the network port."},{"type":"prose","html":"Note still decides which instrument sounds: the track you have open in Note is armed. Live is the keyboard. Note is the sound."},{"type":"prose","html":"This routing is one-way MIDI. It does not open a Note Set in Live and it does not send a Live Set back."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["In Live, create or select a MIDI track.","Set that track’s MIDI output to the network session.","Confirm Live Settings still has <strong>Track</strong> enabled on that output port.","Confirm Note has <strong>Network Session 1</strong> on and a destination track open.","Do not play yet if you want a clean 10.4.6 listen — or play one note to preview.","Write the Live track name and the output port you chose."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What do you route to the network session? <strong>Answer:</strong> A MIDI track’s output.","Who makes the sound? <strong>Answer:</strong> The armed track in Note.","Does this routing transfer the Live Set into Note? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Live Sets cannot be opened in Note."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.3.3","Note Compatibility / Note FAQ (Live Sets cannot be opened in Note — contrast)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Route one empty or simple MIDI track to the network session. Leave it ready for 10.4.6."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l10-4-5</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l10-4-6","title":"10.4.6 — Play Live → hear/play Note instruments","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.4.6 — Play Live → hear/play Note instruments","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 10 · Module 10.4 — Network MIDI from Mac / Live</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can play notes in Live and hear Note’s instrument on the iPhone or iPad."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The manual’s last sentence on this path: you can now play notes in Live and the signal will be sent to Note. That is the test. Play Live’s MIDI keyboard, a clip, or an external controller into that Live track. Note’s open track sounds."},{"type":"prose","html":"If you hear nothing, walk the chain backward: Note track open, <strong>Network Session 1</strong> on, same Wi-Fi, Mac session connected, Live <strong>Track</strong> enabled, Live track output aimed at the session. Do not add Cloud or Link Audio as a MIDI fix. Link Audio sends audio from Note (2.0+, send only). This lesson sends MIDI the other way."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Capture</strong> in Note still records what arrives, including pitch, length, velocity, and aftertouch if present. MPE, pitch-bend, and sustain stay unsupported."},{"type":"prose","html":"Syllabus practice for the module: one successful Live→Note MIDI note."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a melodic or Drum Rack track in Note.","In Live, record-arm the MIDI track that outputs to the network session.","Play a note in Live. Listen on the iOS device (wired headphones if you are judging timing).","If silent, check the chain in Instruction. Change only one link at a time.","Play a short phrase and <strong>Capture</strong> it in Note.","Stop. Write pass/fail for “heard one note.”"],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What proves this path works? <strong>Answer:</strong> A note played in Live is heard from the armed Note instrument.","Why would Link Audio not fix silence here? <strong>Answer:</strong> Link Audio sends Note audio to Live/Push. This lesson sends MIDI from Live to Note.","How do you keep the phrase? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Capture</strong> in Note."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.3.3","Note MIDI Input (Recording; Types of MIDI Input)","Note Compatibility (Link Audio send only — contrast)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Get one Live note to sound in Note. That is the module practice. If you have no Mac, write blocked."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l10-4-6</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l10-4-7","title":"10.4.7 — Live clip drives Note Drift","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.4.7 — Live clip drives Note Drift","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 10 · Module 10.4 — Network MIDI from Mac / Live</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can let a Live MIDI clip play Note’s Drift so the computer sequences the iOS synth."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"This is the network MIDI lab. Same chain as 10.4.6, with a clip in Live instead of only live playing. Put a short MIDI clip on the Live track whose output is the network session. Open a <strong>Drift</strong> track in Note. Launch the Live clip. Drift plays."},{"type":"prose","html":"Drift is Note’s subtractive synth. You are not moving the Set to Live. You are using Live as a sequencer for a sound that still lives in Note. If you also want tempo lock, enable <strong>Ableton Link</strong> on both sides — optional, and a different feature. <strong>Sync Start/Stop</strong> would couple transport; leave it off if you want to launch independently."},{"type":"prose","html":"Capture in Note if you want the notes stored in the Note Set. Otherwise Note is just the sound module. Either use is valid. MIDI files still cannot be imported or exported."},{"type":"prose","html":"If you have no Mac or no Live, document the block. Do not invent a passing lab."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["In Note, open a Drift track. Enable <strong>Network Session 1</strong>.","In Live, put a 1- or 2-bar MIDI clip on the routed track. Enable <strong>Track</strong> on the network output.","Launch the Live clip. Listen to Drift.","Optionally enable Link if you want a shared tempo, then launch again.","Optionally <strong>Capture</strong> in Note so the phrase exists as a Note clip.","Stop both. Write pass/fail. Repeat the open-Drift step from memory."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What is Live doing in this lab? <strong>Answer:</strong> Sending MIDI (the clip) over the network session.","What is Note doing? <strong>Answer:</strong> Playing Drift on the armed track, and optionally Capturing the notes.","Does this create a Live Set you can reopen in Note? <strong>Answer:</strong> No."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.3.3","Ableton Note Manual (Drift as subtractive instrument — course locked fact / instrument chapters)","Note FAQ (no MIDI file I/O; no Live→Note Sets)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Drive Drift with one Live clip, or write that the Mac/Live path is unavailable. Keep the written pass/fail with the 10.4.6 note."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l10-4-7</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"note-m-10-5-105","code":"10.5","title":"10.5","blurb":"10.5","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"note-l10-5-1","title":"10.5.1 — Apps appear in MIDI device list","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.5.1 — Apps appear in MIDI device list","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 10 · Module 10.5 — MIDI from other apps</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can make another iOS music app appear in Note’s MIDI device list by opening it."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Note can receive MIDI from other apps on the same device. Compatible apps appear in the list of available MIDI devices. The Knowledge Base: open the other app and it automatically appears in the list on the MIDI Input page. Examples the KB names: chord creators, arpeggiators, and other iOS music apps."},{"type":"prose","html":"You do not AirDrop a MIDI file. You do not enable AUv3 — Note cannot host Audio Unit v3 devices (FAQ: that would break Live compatibility on Windows). Inter-app MIDI is a toggle on a named app, not a plugin scan."},{"type":"prose","html":"If the other app is not open, it may not appear. The extracts do not promise a persistent ghost name for every sequencer on the phone. Open the app, then look at Note’s list."},{"type":"prose","html":"This is the same list as USB, Bluetooth, and <strong>Network Session 1</strong>. Each row is a source."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a second iOS music app that can send MIDI (if you own one).","Switch back to Note. Open <strong>MIDI Settings → Input</strong>.","Find the other app’s name in the available devices list.","If it is missing, return to the other app, confirm it is running, and reopen Note’s list.","Do not enable the toggle yet (10.5.2).","If you own no sending app, write that. The list may only show hardware and Network Session."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["How does another app get into Note’s list? <strong>Answer:</strong> Open it. It appears automatically on the MIDI Input page.","Is that AUv3 hosting? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Note does not host AUv3.","Can you import a MIDI clip file from that app instead? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. MIDI clips cannot be imported or exported."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.3.4","Note MIDI Input (To allow input from other apps)","Note FAQ (no AUv3; no MIDI file I/O)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Screenshot the MIDI list with the other app named, or write “no sending app installed.”"},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l10-5-1</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l10-5-2","title":"10.5.2 — Enable app toggle","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.5.2 — Enable app toggle","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 10 · Module 10.5 — MIDI from other apps</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can enable the other app’s toggle in Note so its MIDI can reach the armed track."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"To receive MIDI from an app, enable the toggle next to its name. The manual’s caption: “A Connected App.” Same enable pattern as a keyboard. The app must already be in the list (open it first)."},{"type":"prose","html":"The toggle in Note is only half of the handshake. The next lesson: you may need to enable MIDI output in the other app’s settings. If Note’s toggle is on and you still hear silence, look there before you remap macros."},{"type":"prose","html":"Once both sides send, open a Note track and play or run the other app. <strong>Capture</strong> records the notes. Types and unsupported messages do not change."},{"type":"prose","html":"Disable the toggle before you close Note if you do not want that app enabled next time (Manual §2.3)."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Confirm the other app is listed.","Enable its toggle in Note.","Open a Note track.","Trigger a note or pattern in the other app if its output is already on. If silent, leave that for 10.5.3.","Disable and re-enable the toggle once so you feel the switch.","Find the row again from memory."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does the app toggle do? <strong>Answer:</strong> It allows that app to send MIDI to Note.","Is Note’s toggle always enough? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. The other app may need MIDI output enabled in its own settings.","How do you avoid the app staying live next session? <strong>Answer:</strong> Switch the toggle off before closing Note."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.3.4 and §2.3","Note MIDI Input (enable the other iOS app using its toggle)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Turn the app toggle on. Leave it ready for 10.5.3. If no app exists, write unavailable."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l10-5-2</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l10-5-3","title":"10.5.3 — App-side MIDI output requirements","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.5.3 — App-side MIDI output requirements","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 10 · Module 10.5 — MIDI from other apps</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can check the other app’s own settings for MIDI output when Note’s toggle is already on."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Depending on the app, you may need to enable MIDI output in its settings. That is the entire official sentence. Note cannot flip a hidden switch inside a third-party sequencer. Chord creators and arpeggiators are the KB’s examples; each vendor labels the output differently."},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} The extracts do not name a specific third-party app’s menu. Do not invent “Background Audio + Virtual MIDI” as a universal Note requirement. Use the other app’s own MIDI output control, then return to Note."},{"type":"prose","html":"If output is off, Note’s list can still show the app and the toggle can be on, and you will hear nothing. That is not a broken Drum Rack. After output is on, play or run the pattern and <strong>Capture</strong> in Note."},{"type":"prose","html":"Note still will not import that app’s project file or a <code>.mid</code> export. Live notes only."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Keep Note’s app toggle on.","Switch to the other app. Open its settings and find MIDI output / virtual MIDI / send MIDI — whatever it actually labels.","Enable sending. Point it at Note if the app lists destinations.","Return to Note. Open a track. Trigger the other app.","If you still hear silence, confirm both toggles and that the Note track is open. Do not add AUv3; Note cannot host it.","Capture a short phrase if notes arrive."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What extra step may the other app require? <strong>Answer:</strong> Enable MIDI output in that app’s settings.","Does a name in Note’s list prove the other app is sending? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. The list is presence. Sending is its output plus Note’s toggle.","Can you load that app’s MIDI file into Note instead? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. No MIDI file import."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.3.4","Note MIDI Input (app-side MIDI output)","Note FAQ (no MIDI I/O; no AUv3)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Write the other app’s exact output control name as you found it, or write that you do not own a sending app."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l10-5-3</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l10-5-4","title":"10.5.4 — sequencer app → Note (if owned)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.5.4 — sequencer app → Note (if owned)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 10 · Module 10.5 — MIDI from other apps</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can drive a Note instrument from another iOS sequencer if you own one, or honestly skip the lab."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"This is the inter-app lab. Open the sequencer or arpeggiator, let it appear in Note’s MIDI list, enable its toggle, enable that app’s MIDI output, open a Note track, run a pattern, <strong>Capture</strong> if you want the notes in the Set."},{"type":"prose","html":"Syllabus practice is optional. Owning a second music app is not a Gate I requirement. Gate I is a Link session <strong>or</strong> a MIDI controller with at least one macro plus <strong>Capture</strong> mapped. An app-to-Note jam is extra literacy."},{"type":"prose","html":"If you do not own a sending app, write that and stop. Do not download a random AUv3 host as a workaround — Note does not host AUv3. Do not pretend a YouTube MIDI file imported; it cannot."},{"type":"prose","html":"Prefer wired headphones while you judge timing."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["If you have no sending app, write “not owned” and skip to Self-check.","Open the app. Confirm it in Note’s list. Enable the toggle. Enable its MIDI output.","Open Drift, Wavetable, Sampler, or a Drum Rack in Note.","Run a one- or two-bar pattern. Listen.","<strong>Capture</strong> the result. Glance at the MIDI Editor.","Disable Note’s app toggle if you do not want it next session."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Is this lab required for Gate I? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Gate I is Link <strong>or</strong> controller mapping (macro + <strong>Capture</strong>).","What if you do not own a sequencer app? <strong>Answer:</strong> Document that. The module practice is optional.","What recording method stores the notes? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Capture</strong>."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.3.4","Note MIDI Input","Course syllabus Module 10.5 practice; Gate I (Level 10)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Capture one inter-app phrase, or file “not owned.” Either is a complete exercise."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l10-5-4</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"note-m-10-6-106","code":"10.6","title":"10.6","blurb":"10.6","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"note-l10-6-1","title":"10.6.1 — Map up to 8 macros: tap macro → move hardware","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.6.1 — Map up to 8 macros: tap macro → move hardware","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 10 · Module 10.6 — MIDI mapping &amp; expression</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can map a hardware knob or fader to a Note macro by tapping the macro and moving the control."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can assign up to eight macros and map key parameters to a connected MIDI controller (Manual §2.3.5). RN 1.0.15: MIDI mapping for actions plus 8 macros. Open mapping from <strong>App Settings</strong> (three-dot menu, bottom left) → <strong>MIDI Settings → Mapping</strong> (KB), which sits with <strong>Link &amp; MIDI</strong> in the manual."},{"type":"prose","html":"To create a macro map: tap a macro knob in Note, then tweak the control on the connected controller. The KB says the same: tap a macro dial, tweak a parameter on the hardware. That is the entire learn gesture. You do not type CC numbers in the extracts."},{"type":"prose","html":"Macros are the surface you already use on Track Presets (instrument plus two audio effects). Mapping them means you can ride filter, decay, or whatever that preset exposes without looking at the glass. Eight is the cap. Do not invent a ninth."},{"type":"prose","html":"A controller must already be connected and enabled (USB, Bluetooth, network, or app). Mapping an unarmed, disabled source will not take."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Connect and enable a MIDI controller.","Open <strong>Settings → MIDI Settings → Mapping</strong>.","Open a track that shows at least one macro.","Tap a macro knob. Move one hardware knob or fader.","Move the hardware again and watch the macro. If it does not follow, repeat the tap-then-tweak learn.","Map a second macro if you have time. Count them. Stop at eight."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["How many macros can you map? <strong>Answer:</strong> Up to eight.","What is the learn gesture? <strong>Answer:</strong> Tap the macro in Note, then tweak the hardware control.","Where do you open mapping? <strong>Answer:</strong> Three-dot <strong>App Settings</strong> → <strong>MIDI Settings → Mapping</strong> (Link &amp; MIDI)."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.3.5","Note MIDI Input (MIDI Mapping)","Note Release Notes 1.0.15 (actions + 8 macros)","Ableton Note.md §1.3 MIDI Mapping"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Map one macro and ride it through a four-bar loop. Leave the map in place for 10.6.7."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l10-6-1</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l10-6-2","title":"10.6.2 — Map Capture","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.6.2 — Map Capture","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 10 · Module 10.6 — MIDI mapping &amp; expression</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can map <strong>Capture</strong> to a hardware button so you can store a take without touching the screen."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Besides macros, you can map several common controls: <strong>Capture</strong>, <strong>Clear Capture</strong>, <strong>Start/Stop</strong>, <strong>Quantize</strong>, <strong>Undo</strong>, and <strong>Redo</strong>. To map one, tap its dedicated <strong>Map</strong> button in Note, then tweak (or press) the control on the MIDI device. The KB: tap a <strong>Map</strong> button and press a button on the controller."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Capture</strong> is how Note records MIDI — pads or hardware, Fixed or Free Length. Mapping it is the performance version of that fact. Gate I accepts a MIDI controller with at least one macro <strong>and</strong> <strong>Capture</strong> mapped (or a Link session). This button is half of that hardware path."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Capture</strong> is not <strong>Clear Capture</strong>. Mapping the wrong one drops the buffer or the take you meant to keep. Next lesson is the clear. Today, learn only <strong>Capture</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"Works with USB, Bluetooth, or any enabled source that has a spare button. Note still will not map sustain or pitch-bend because those messages are not supported."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>MIDI Settings → Mapping</strong> with a controller enabled.","Tap the <strong>Map</strong> button for <strong>Capture</strong>.","Press one pad or button on the hardware.","Open a track. Play a phrase. Hit the mapped button instead of the on-screen <strong>Capture</strong>.","Confirm a clip appeared (Free Length) or the take landed in the Fixed Length clip.","Repeat the learn once from memory if the first button was awkward."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["How do you assign <strong>Capture</strong>? <strong>Answer:</strong> Tap its <strong>Map</strong> button, then press a hardware control.","Why does Gate I care about this map? <strong>Answer:</strong> The hardware path of Gate I is ≥1 macro plus <strong>Capture</strong> mapped (or you use Link instead).","Does mapping <strong>Capture</strong> enable MPE or sustain? <strong>Answer:</strong> No."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.3.5","Note MIDI Input (MIDI Mapping)","Note FAQ (record MIDI via Capture)","Course syllabus Gate I"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Map <strong>Capture</strong> to one button and store a take without touching the Capture control on screen."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l10-6-2</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l10-6-3","title":"10.6.3 — Map Clear Capture","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.6.3 — Map Clear Capture","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 10 · Module 10.6 — MIDI mapping &amp; expression</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can map <strong>Clear Capture</strong> to a different button from <strong>Capture</strong> so you can dump a bad buffer."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Clear Capture</strong> is one of the dedicated map targets next to <strong>Capture</strong>, <strong>Start/Stop</strong>, <strong>Quantize</strong>, <strong>Undo</strong>, and <strong>Redo</strong>. Learn it the same way: tap <strong>Map</strong> on <strong>Clear Capture</strong>, then press a hardware control."},{"type":"prose","html":"Capture Timeout (Preferences) already decides whether an uncaptured buffer expires after you stop playing (default on) or waits until you play again (off). <strong>Clear Capture</strong> is the manual dump. {type:'gap'} The mapping page names the action; the extracts do not print a longer definition of <strong>Clear Capture</strong> beyond that name. Treat it as the dedicated clear sitting beside <strong>Capture</strong>, and verify on your install what the button does to an uncaptured buffer."},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not map <strong>Clear Capture</strong> and <strong>Capture</strong> to the same pad. You will lose takes. Put them on opposite hands or on two clearly different buttons."},{"type":"prose","html":"This map is not required for Gate I. <strong>Capture</strong> is. Learn <strong>Clear Capture</strong> so a flubbed phrase does not sit under your fingers."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>Mapping</strong>. Confirm <strong>Capture</strong> is already mapped to button A.","Tap <strong>Map</strong> on <strong>Clear Capture</strong>. Press button B, not A.","Play a phrase you do not want. Press <strong>Clear Capture</strong> on the hardware.","Play a phrase you do want. Press <strong>Capture</strong>.","Listen. The second phrase should be the clip, not the first.","Write which two buttons you used."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["How do you learn <strong>Clear Capture</strong>? <strong>Answer:</strong> Tap its <strong>Map</strong> button, then press a hardware control.","Why use a different button from <strong>Capture</strong>? <strong>Answer:</strong> Same pad would clear or capture by accident.","Is <strong>Clear Capture</strong> required for Gate I? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Gate I names <strong>Capture</strong> (plus a macro) or Link."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.3.5","Note MIDI Input (MIDI Mapping)","Ableton Note Manual §2.4.2 (Capture Timeout — related buffer behavior)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Map <strong>Clear Capture</strong> to a second button. Throw away one bad phrase and keep one good phrase without touching the screen."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l10-6-3</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l10-6-4","title":"10.6.4 — Map Start/Stop","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.6.4 — Map Start/Stop","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 10 · Module 10.6 — MIDI mapping &amp; expression</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can map <strong>Start/Stop</strong> to a hardware button so transport is under your hand."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Start/Stop</strong> is a dedicated map target on the MIDI mapping page. Tap its <strong>Map</strong> button, then press a control on the connected controller. The same list holds <strong>Capture</strong>, <strong>Clear Capture</strong>, <strong>Quantize</strong>, <strong>Undo</strong>, and <strong>Redo</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"This mapping starts and stops Note. It is not <strong>Sync Start/Stop</strong> on the Link page. Link’s toggle shares transport with other Link peers. This map is local: a button on your controller toggles Note’s play/stop. You can use both. Do not confuse the labels."},{"type":"prose","html":"Clips still launch on the nearest downbeat when you tap them. Mapped <strong>Start/Stop</strong> is the global transport, the same family as the play/stop control in Session View."},{"type":"prose","html":"Automation still requires transport running. A mapped start is a valid way to get that running state before you ride a knob and tap <strong>Add</strong>."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>MIDI Settings → Mapping</strong>.","Tap <strong>Map</strong> next to <strong>Start/Stop</strong>. Press a spare button (not your <strong>Capture</strong> button).","Return to Session View. Press the button to start. Press it to stop.","Launch a clip, then stop from the hardware. Listen for the stop.","If Link <strong>Sync Start/Stop</strong> is also on, note that other peers may follow — that is Link, not this map.","Write which button is transport."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["How do you assign <strong>Start/Stop</strong>? <strong>Answer:</strong> Tap its <strong>Map</strong> button, then press a hardware control.","How is this different from Link <strong>Sync Start/Stop</strong>? <strong>Answer:</strong> The map is a local Note transport button. The Link toggle shares start/stop with peers.","Why does automation care? <strong>Answer:</strong> Transport must be running to capture automation."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.3.5","Note MIDI Input (MIDI Mapping)","Ableton Note Manual §2.2.1 (Sync Start/Stop — contrast)","Capture in Note (transport must be running for automation)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Map <strong>Start/Stop</strong>. Start, launch a clip, stop, all without touching Note’s play button."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l10-6-4</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l10-6-5","title":"10.6.5 — Map Quantize","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.6.5 — Map Quantize","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 10 · Module 10.6 — MIDI mapping &amp; expression</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can map <strong>Quantize</strong> to a hardware button and use it as a timing decision, not a reflex."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Quantize</strong> is one of the six dedicated action maps. Learn it: tap <strong>Map</strong>, press a button. RN 1.0.15 added quantization amount on the editor side. The MIDI Editor’s Quantization tab snaps notes to a beat value with an Amount slider. There is also a global Quantization button in Instrument View (Ableton Note.md). The mapped action fires that command family from hardware."},{"type":"prose","html":"Quantize after a take if the idea is right and the time is sloppy. Do not map it next to <strong>Capture</strong> and mash both every bar. Groove Amount (Set Settings, 1.0.15) and per-clip swing still exist; slamming 100% quantize fights the feel you set there."},{"type":"prose","html":"This map is not required for Gate I. It is how you keep your eyes on the pads while you tighten a clip."},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} The extracts do not spell the exact grid value the mapped <strong>Quantize</strong> applies. Check on your install what the button does, then refine in the MIDI Editor if you need a different amount."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Capture a slightly loose phrase on purpose.","Open <strong>Mapping</strong>. Assign <strong>Quantize</strong> to a button that is not <strong>Capture</strong> or <strong>Start/Stop</strong>.","Press the mapped button. Listen.","Open the MIDI Editor Quantization tab if you need a different beat value or Amount.","Compare to Groove Amount in <strong>Set Settings</strong> if the clip now feels stiff.","Write which button is <strong>Quantize</strong>."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Where do you learn the <strong>Quantize</strong> action? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>MIDI Settings → Mapping</strong>, tap <strong>Map</strong>, press a hardware control.","Does mapping <strong>Quantize</strong> replace the MIDI Editor tab? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. The editor still has beat value and Amount (and RN 1.0.15 amount).","Should you hit <strong>Quantize</strong> on every <strong>Capture</strong>? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Use it when timing should snap. Groove/swing may be the point."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.3.5","Note MIDI Input (MIDI Mapping)","Note Release Notes 1.0.15 (Quantisation amount)","Ableton Note.md (global Quantization button; Amount slider)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Map <strong>Quantize</strong>. A/B one clip before and after the button. Keep the version that still feels like the idea."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l10-6-5</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l10-6-6","title":"10.6.6 — Map Undo / Redo","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.6.6 — Map Undo / Redo","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 10 · Module 10.6 — MIDI mapping &amp; expression</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can map <strong>Undo</strong> and <strong>Redo</strong> to two hardware controls and use them as a safety pair."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Undo</strong> and <strong>Redo</strong> are the last two dedicated action maps in Manual §2.3.5. Learn each with its <strong>Map</strong> button and a distinct hardware control. On screen, Undo/Redo arrows undo or redo any changes you make in the Set (manual, Instrument View chrome). The maps are those actions on buttons."},{"type":"prose","html":"Use them after a bad <strong>Capture</strong>, a mistaken <strong>Quantize</strong>, a macro ride you did not mean to <strong>Add</strong> as automation, or a deleted note. Map them away from <strong>Capture</strong> and <strong>Clear Capture</strong> so a palm hit does not walk the history."},{"type":"prose","html":"There is no MIDI file revert and no Cloud version browser in the extracts. Undo/Redo is the local safety net. Auto-save still runs every ~10–15 seconds; that is crash resilience, not an undo stack you browse."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>Mapping</strong>. Map <strong>Undo</strong> to one button and <strong>Redo</strong> to another.","Capture a clip. Delete a note or quantize it.","Press <strong>Undo</strong> on the hardware. Confirm the change reversed.","Press <strong>Redo</strong>. Confirm it returned.","Play a phrase, <strong>Capture</strong>, <strong>Undo</strong> the capture, <strong>Redo</strong> it.","Write the two buttons. Keep them for the rest of the session."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["How many dedicated action maps include history? <strong>Answer:</strong> Two: <strong>Undo</strong> and <strong>Redo</strong>.","Are they the same as <strong>Clear Capture</strong>? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. <strong>Clear Capture</strong> is its own map. Undo/Redo walk Set changes.","Does Cloud give you an undo history? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Cloud is transfer, not backup."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.3.5","Note MIDI Input (Undo named among mappable actions)","Ableton Note Manual (Undo/Redo arrows in Instrument View)","Note FAQ (auto-save 10–15 seconds; Cloud is not backup)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Map <strong>Undo</strong> and <strong>Redo</strong>. Deliberately ruin a clip, undo, redo, then leave the clip as you want it."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l10-6-6</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l10-6-7","title":"10.6.7 — Performance mapping strategy (Capture + 4 macros minimum)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.6.7 — Performance mapping strategy (Capture + 4 macros minimum)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 10 · Module 10.6 — MIDI mapping &amp; expression</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can build a small performance map: <strong>Capture</strong> plus at least four macros, without exceeding eight macros."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The syllabus practice for this module is four macros plus <strong>Capture</strong>, or a Magic Keyboard workflow. The product cap is eight macros plus the action set: <strong>Capture</strong>, <strong>Clear Capture</strong>, <strong>Start/Stop</strong>, <strong>Quantize</strong>, <strong>Undo</strong>, <strong>Redo</strong>. A strategy is a choice of which of those you can hit without looking."},{"type":"prose","html":"Minimum useful kit: <strong>Capture</strong> (store the take), four macros that change the sound you are actually playing (filter, decay, send, a second FX). Add <strong>Clear Capture</strong> and <strong>Start/Stop</strong> if you have buttons left. Leave <strong>Quantize</strong> off the most comfortable pad so you do not harden every loop by accident."},{"type":"prose","html":"This is not a brand template. Note does not ship a “performance mode” beyond Mapping. Track Preset still means instrument plus two audio effects; macros are whatever that preset exposes. If a Wavetable preset later opens in Live Lite/Intro/Standard as a locked rack, those same macros are what remain editable — another reason to learn them now."},{"type":"prose","html":"Gate I hardware path: at least one macro and <strong>Capture</strong>. This lesson asks for four macros so the hand learns a real surface."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Enable one controller. Open <strong>Mapping</strong>.","Map <strong>Capture</strong> if it is not mapped.","Map four macros you can hear (tap macro, move hardware) on the track you will play.","Optionally map <strong>Clear Capture</strong> and <strong>Start/Stop</strong> to leftover buttons.","Play a phrase, ride two macros, <strong>Capture</strong>, ride again after the clip exists (automation still needs transport + <strong>Add</strong> if you want motion stored).","Count maps. Confirm you are at four macros or more, and not over eight."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What is the sourced macro cap? <strong>Answer:</strong> Eight.","What is the syllabus minimum for this practice? <strong>Answer:</strong> Four macros + <strong>Capture</strong> (or Magic Keyboard instead).","What is the Gate I hardware minimum? <strong>Answer:</strong> ≥1 macro + <strong>Capture</strong> mapped (or a Link session instead)."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.3.5","Note MIDI Input (8 macros; actions)","Note Release Notes 1.0.15","Using Note Presets in Live (macros remain on locked racks)","Course syllabus Module 10.6 practice; Gate I"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Leave a four-macro + <strong>Capture</strong> map on your controller. Play one take using only those controls plus the keys or pads."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l10-6-7</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l10-6-8","title":"10.6.8 — Polyphonic aftertouch from controllers","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.6.8 — Polyphonic aftertouch from controllers","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 10 · Module 10.6 — MIDI mapping &amp; expression</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can play polyphonic aftertouch into Note from a controller that sends it, and you can name what still will not record."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Note’s MIDI input records pitch, length, velocity, and aftertouch, including polyphonic aftertouch. It does not record MPE, pitch-bend, or sustain. RN 1.2.1: polyphonic aftertouch from external MIDI, and Y-axis on Keys in In-Key mode for supported presets."},{"type":"prose","html":"Poly AT is per-note pressure. MPE is a different spec and is explicitly not supported. A controller that only speaks MPE or pitch-bend will not get those extras into the clip. Velocity still records. Aftertouch that the device sends as poly AT can record as of 1.2.1."},{"type":"prose","html":"Check <strong>Settings → Help</strong> for 1.2.1 or later before you blame the keyboard. The track must be open (armed). <strong>Capture</strong> stores the performance. {type:'gap'} The extracts do not list which factory presets respond musically to poly AT, except “supported presets” for the Keys Y-axis. Listen."},{"type":"prose","html":"This is expression, not a ninth macro. You do not map aftertouch on the Mapping page in the sourced steps; you play it."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Confirm Note is 1.2.1+ (<strong>Settings → Help</strong>).","Enable a controller that can send polyphonic aftertouch. Open a melodic track.","Play a chord or two notes and press into them independently if the hardware allows.","<strong>Capture</strong> the take. Listen back for pressure motion.","Try a pitch-bend or sustain gesture if the hardware has those controls. Confirm they do not become clip data.","Write pass/fail for poly AT heard, and “not supported” for bend/sustain."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Which expression types are recorded? <strong>Answer:</strong> Pitch, length, velocity, aftertouch including polyphonic aftertouch.","Which are not? <strong>Answer:</strong> MPE, pitch-bend, sustain.","When did poly AT from external MIDI land? <strong>Answer:</strong> Note 1.2.1."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note MIDI Input (Types of MIDI Input)","Note Release Notes 1.2.1 (polyphonic aftertouch; Keys Y-axis)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Capture one phrase that uses pressure if your controller can send poly AT. If it cannot, write that and capture a velocity-only phrase instead."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l10-6-8</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l10-6-9","title":"10.6.9 — iPad Magic Keyboard: melodic layout like Live","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.6.9 — iPad Magic Keyboard: melodic layout like Live","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 10 · Module 10.6 — MIDI mapping &amp; expression</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can play a melodic Note track from an iPad Magic Keyboard using the Live-style layout added in 1.2.1."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Note 1.2.1 adds Magic Keyboard on iPad: Live-style layout for melodic tracks, and drums mapped 1..4 to Z..V. That is the sourced melodic fact. If you are on iPhone, or on iPad without a Magic Keyboard, or on Note below 1.2.1, this path is unavailable. Check <strong>Settings → Help</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"“Live-style” is the release-note phrase. {type:'gap'} The extract does not print the computer-keyboard note map (which letter is C, and so on). Do not invent a QWERTY chart. Play, listen, and learn the layout on your iPad the way you would in Live’s computer MIDI keyboard — the RN’s comparison — without writing unofficial key lists into this course."},{"type":"prose","html":"The Magic Keyboard is not a class-compliant USB MIDI controller in this lesson; it is an iPad keyboard feature. You still open a track to arm it. You still <strong>Capture</strong>. Pitch-bend and sustain remain unsupported. Mapping macros still wants a MIDI controller if you need knobs."},{"type":"prose","html":"Syllabus practice allows this workflow instead of four macros + <strong>Capture</strong> on hardware."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Confirm iPad, Magic Keyboard attached, Note 1.2.1+.","Open a melodic track (Drift, Wavetable, or Melodic Sampler).","Type on the Magic Keyboard and listen for notes. Move around the layout until you can play a short scale.","<strong>Capture</strong> a phrase (Fixed or Free).","Open the MIDI Editor and confirm the notes.","If you have no Magic Keyboard, write unavailable and keep the controller map from 10.6.7."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does RN 1.2.1 say about Magic Keyboard on iPad for melodic tracks? <strong>Answer:</strong> Live-style layout.","Does this lesson give you an official letter-to-note chart? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. The extract does not include one.","Can you use this as the Module 10.6 practice instead of four macros? <strong>Answer:</strong> Yes. The syllabus allows a Magic Keyboard workflow."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note Release Notes 1.2.1 (Magic Keyboard on iPad, Live-style layout for melodic)","Note MIDI Input (Capture still records MIDI)","Course syllabus Module 10.6 practice"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Capture one melodic clip from the Magic Keyboard, or write that you are on iPhone / no keyboard and will use the controller map instead."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l10-6-9</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l10-6-10","title":"10.6.10 — Magic Keyboard drums 1–4 / Z–V; octave X/Y; arrows tracks; space Capture/transport","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.6.10 — Magic Keyboard drums 1–4 / Z–V; octave X/Y; arrows tracks; space Capture/transpo","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 10 · Module 10.6 — MIDI mapping &amp; expression</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can use the sourced Magic Keyboard drum row (1–4 / Z–V) and you can mark the extra title keys as unsourced."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"RN 1.2.1: Magic Keyboard on iPad, Live-style layout for melodic, drums mapped 1..4 to Z..V. That drum sentence is the only drum map in the assigned extracts. Open a Drum Rack. The number row 1–4 and the letter row Z–V hit kit cells. Play them. <strong>Capture</strong> the groove."},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} Octave X/Y, arrow keys changing tracks, and space for <strong>Capture</strong>/transport are in the lesson title but not in the Note Release Notes extract, the manual, or Note MIDI Input. Do not teach those bindings as Ableton facts. If you discover them on your iPad, label the note “observed, not in extracts.”"},{"type":"prose","html":"iPhone has no Magic Keyboard path in the RN line. Below 1.2.1, the feature is absent. A USB or Bluetooth pad controller remains the documented drum input."},{"type":"prose","html":"This lesson completes the keyboard workflow option for Module 10.6 practice."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Confirm iPad + Magic Keyboard + Note 1.2.1+.","Open a Drum Rack track.","Play keys <strong>1–4</strong> and <strong>Z–V</strong>. Listen for four-plus-four hits on the kit.","Capture a 1- or 2-bar loop.","Do not write X/Y, arrows, or space as official maps. If you test them, mark results as observation only.","Return to a melodic track and confirm the Live-style layout still works (10.6.9)."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What drum mapping does RN 1.2.1 actually state? <strong>Answer:</strong> Drums mapped 1..4 to Z..V.","What must you treat as a gap? <strong>Answer:</strong> Octave X/Y, arrows for tracks, space for Capture/transport.","Where does this feature run? <strong>Answer:</strong> iPad Magic Keyboard, Note 1.2.1+."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note Release Notes 1.2.1","Ableton Note Manual (Drum Rack 16 pads — context)","Capture in Note"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Capture a Drum Rack clip using only <strong>1–4</strong> and <strong>Z–V</strong>, or write that Magic Keyboard is unavailable. Keep any extra-key tests in a separate “observed” note."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l10-6-10</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l10-6-11","title":"10.6.11 — Gate I day","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.6.11 — Gate I day","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 10 · Module 10.6 — MIDI mapping &amp; expression</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can self-score Gate I from the syllabus: a Link session <strong>or</strong> a MIDI controller with at least one macro and <strong>Capture</strong> mapped."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Gate I is the Level 10 checkpoint. The syllabus line is short on purpose: Link <strong>or</strong> MIDI controller with ≥1 macro + <strong>Capture</strong> mapped. You do not need both. You do not need four macros (that was module practice). You do not need Magic Keyboard. You do not need Link Audio. You do not need a second paid app."},{"type":"prose","html":"This desktop course cannot watch your iPhone. You test on your own Set and write pass or fail. If hardware is missing, the Link path is still a full pass: <strong>Ableton Link</strong> on, <strong>Connected Peers</strong> screenshot even at zero, and one sentence on <strong>Sync Start/Stop</strong>. If you have a controller, enable it, map one macro (tap macro, move hardware), map <strong>Capture</strong> (tap <strong>Map</strong>, press a button), and store a take from that button."},{"type":"prose","html":"Failed items send you back to the matching lesson, not to Level 11."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Choose path A (Link) or path B (controller map). You may do both; you need one.","Path A: <strong>Ableton Link</strong> on. Screenshot <strong>Connected Peers</strong>. Write: <strong>Sync Start/Stop</strong> syncs playback position as well as tempo.","Path B: controller enabled. One macro follows a knob. <strong>Capture</strong> on a button stores a clip.","Write pass/fail for the path you chose. Repeat any fail before Level 11.","Record the Note version from <strong>Settings → Help</strong>.","Keep the screenshot or the mapped-take clip with this page."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What are the two legal ways to pass Gate I? <strong>Answer:</strong> A Link session, <strong>or</strong> a MIDI controller with ≥1 macro and <strong>Capture</strong> mapped.","Do you need Link Audio or four macros? <strong>Answer:</strong> No.","What if a criterion fails? <strong>Answer:</strong> Repeat the matching lesson. Do not start Level 11 on a fail."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Course syllabus Gate I (section 3; Module 10.6 checkpoint)","Ableton Note Manual §2.2.1 and §2.3.5","Note MIDI Input (Mapping)","Using Link in Note"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Fill a two-line scorecard:"},{"type":"list","items":["Path used: Link / controller / both","Result: pass / fail — and the evidence (peer screenshot or mapped Capture take)"],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not open Level 11 until the line says pass."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l10-6-11</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]}]};
